, \uc2 Indofood\uc2  Agri (5JS.SG) +3.5% at S$2.66, Wilmar (F34.SG) +0.6% at S$6.44. Index closing in on 3200, may pose N/T hurdle. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes Chinese government's blueprint for its 12th five-year economic plan (2011-15). Expects Chinese consumption to increase and rapidly become more sophisticated in years ahead, looks for investment opportunities among consumer goods-related firms catering to more widespread consumption, firms that could benefit from labor-saving investment. Sees this autumn as historic buying opportunity for Japanese stocks and advocate a bullish stance, mainly due to 1) sharp improvement in corporate earnings, 2) low P/B (around 1.0X), 3) government and BOJ moves to curb yen appreciation, 4) prospective rebound in the US economy from 2011 1H. House is bullish on stocks sensitive to global economy, bearish on defensives, utilities, and public interest-related stocks. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1492.48 midday amid strong regional markets; dealer says possibility of early elections fueling some buying interest in market. "There is some caution though, as the third-quarter earnings aren't likely to be as strong as the first half," adds dealer; says 1495 still acting as strong resistance. Plantation stocks leading gains after CPO price rally; market breadth positive, with 397 gainers vs 261 decliners. Gainers include plantation stocks Tradewinds Plantation (6327.KU) +6.0% at MYR2.30, \uc2 Boustead Holdings\uc2  (2771.KU) +5.1% at MYR5.95; Among decliners, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 1.1% at MYR2.59, Genting (3182.KU) down 0.8% at MYR10.42. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.8% at 448.61 as foreigners buy on hopes more bluechip companies will report solid 3Q results this week. Despite recent correction, 440 support held firm, suggesting benchmark index will resume uptrend this week, says Hanoi-based brokerage analyst; expects foreign fund inflows to continue. Still, reckons investors will tread carefully after government says CPI rose 9.66% on-year in October as a result of higher education costs, pricier housing and building material prices. Volume thin with 28.6 million shares valued at VND735 billion traded. Among actives, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends down 0.6% at VND15,300, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends flat at VND25,300, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) ends +0.4% at VND24,200. Tips 450-455 range tomorrow. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY little changed midday after PBOC sets higher-than-expected fixing as some anticipated it around 6.6650 today due to weaker USD/global (today's at 6.6729 vs 6.6759 Friday), mild language on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue from G-20 over weekend. USD/CNY at 6.6564 vs 6.6567 last close; tipped in 6.6500-6.6650 band for remainder of session. "We don't see any substantial progress made during the G-20 and there shouldn't be substantial impact on China's (\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) policies," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. "The market's expectation for further yuan appreciation is still high, but now that the dollar-yuan central parity rate was set higher in recent days, the market basically lacks momentum to move either way," she says. Adds, downward pressure may rise afternoon on USD/CNY as trading desks refrain from holding any long USD positions overnight, exporters' CNY demand usually surface afternoon. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.9% at 23,738.09 midday, bolstered by A-shares' strength with Shanghai Composite +1.3% at interval; volume solid at HK$48.82 billion. HK market fell 1.0% last week due to China's unexpected rate hike, snapping 7-week winning streak. Still, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  feels rate hike "actually removes overhangs and could be a market multiple-expansion catalyst." HKEx (0388.HK) leads blue-chips' gains, +4.4% at HK$181.10 as SGX/ASX merger news kindles M&A interest. Coal stocks also strong after Yanzhou Coal's (1171.HK) positive earnings guidance; Shenhua (1088.HK) +3.9% at HK$36.30, China Coal (1898.HK) +4.1% at HK$14.90. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0428 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sustains losses at 1,116.4 vs 1,123.0 late Friday in Seoul on solid Kospi gains (+1.1%) amid strong foreign buying in local stocks and rising euro, traders say. Local bank trader pegs support at 1,112 for day. Says pair may extend falls later as "support for the dollar around these levels doesn't really seem firm," while extent of any USD buying smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to weaken after G-20 nations agreed on need to avoid "competitive devaluation" of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In recent months, local authorities suspected of frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem KRW's rise, according to traders. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] India's 7.99% 2017 bond may attract most foreign fund interest when incremental $5 billion worth of government bond limits allocated by securities regulator, says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . Notes 7-year notes have least duration risk among liquid bonds. Adds, 10-year note beginning to turn illiquid, forcing yield higher than more liquid 12-year bond. Spread between 7-year, 12-year bond not attractive enough to justify scaling duration risk. Holds recommendation for long 7-year note on view foreign funds likely to target bonds with low duration, relative liquidity. But house continue to expect RBI to hike repo, reverse repo rate by 25 bps each at policy meeting on Nov. 2 given hawkish tone it has taken on inflation, which it has described as "unacceptably high." (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +0.1% at 9439.43, dipping in and out of negative territory in listless, low volume trading. "The fact that nothing concrete was decided at G-20 shows that it's hard both for Japan to carry out an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and for the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to carry out large-scale monetary easing that would draw criticism of inducing a weaker dollar," says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. Adds trading expected to lack direction until November 2-3 U.S. FOMC meeting, which may set future trend for USD/JPY (now 81.04). Pegs near-term Nikkei support at 9300. Just 11/33 Topix subindexes higher (Topix down 0.1%). Natural resource-related issues rise; \uc2 Mitsui & Co\uc2  (8031.TO) up 1.9% at Y1,315, Inpex (1605.TO) up 2.1% at Y440,000. Real estate shares down on profit-taking; \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) down 1.4% at Y1,578, Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) down 1.1% at Y1,479. \uc2 JVC Kenwood Holdings\uc2  (6632.TO) up 8.9% at Y306 on Nomura Securities upgrade to Buy.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.5% at 20,265.23, helped by buying in select bluechips, gains in most regional markets. Market breadth positive with 25 of 30 index issues up; Hindalco (500440.BY) +1.6% at INR215.30 while Wipro (507685.BY) down 1.7% at INR441. "The start is better than expected. Metals and select heavyweights like \uc2 Hindustan Unilever\uc2  are seeing buying interest before quarterly earnings," says dealer. Adds, liquidity remains strong. Tips Sensex in 20,200-20,350 range. \uc2 Hindustan Unilever\uc2  (500696.BY) +1.1% at INR304.80, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) +0.9% at INR1,090.60. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.3% at INR1,167.50, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) +1.0% at INR1,142.80. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell volatility in USD/JPY market with mildly bearish directional bias to year-end to exploit overpriced USD/JPY gamma and slow spot downtrend via ratio USD/JPY put spreads or, as limited-risk alternative, long reverse knockout USD/JPY puts, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd; adds, short USD down one-touches would suit those who prefer to earn premium. Recommends selling $40 million 2-month 78.90 USD/JPY put, and buying $20 million 81.10 USD/JPY put for zero net premium; or buying Nov 25 81.10 USD/JPY put with 78.20 knockout for 0.25% USD; or selling Nov 25 78.20 one-touch for 28% USD. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Sell Pricey Front-End Dollar/Yen Volatility. (murugesan.suppayyan@dowjones.com) [ 10-25-10 0055ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101025e6ap0002r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
