\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(8/12) Markets Tumble On Growth Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 1,547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2010\par 20:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Alex Frangos, Andrew Monahan and Dave Kansas \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors sprinted toward safety Wednesday as fears about a global economic slowdown intensified, driving the yen briefly to a 15-year high against the dollar and sending stock prices sharply lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Despite the momentary high for the yen, both the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefited from the flight from riskier investments. The two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained more than 2% against the euro and about 1.25% against the pound. Along with the dollar and yen, U.S. Treasurys, German bunds and U.K. gilts, all classic safety plays, jumped higher. Gold popped back above $1,200 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Much of the recent good economic news in the euro zone, the U.K. and Japan has stemmed from strong export data. But with China showing increasing signs of slowing and the U.S. Federal Reserve digging once more into its emergency tool kit to goose an uneven U.S. recovery, the prospect of robust export-driven growth seems less strong than it did just a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 That shift in sentiment has, in turn, prompted a broad flight to safety in the financial markets and a retreat from stocks and other riskier assets. On Wednesday, the Nikkei fell 2.7% in Japan, the Stoxx Europe 600 lost 2%, and large European markets and the U.S. were all off more than 2%. The Dow Jones Industrial Average, down 2.1%, fell into negative territory for the year during early afternoon trading in New York.\par \par \uc2 Underscoring the market jitters, the cost of credit-default insurance among periphery euro-zone countries continued to rise sharply, renewing fears about the fiscal health of the euro zone. Since Aug. 3, sovereign-debt default insurance costs have risen 37% for Ireland, 36% for Italy, 24% for Portugal and 20% for Spain, according to data provider Markit.\par \par \uc2 With government bond yields, which move in the opposite direction of their price, dropping to another round of lows and the specter of deflation rising, chatter about a replay of Japan's so-called lost decades for the economies of Western Europe and the U.S. has resurfaced with greater intensity.\par \par \uc2 "The comparisons to Japan keep coming back," said David Owen, chief European financial economist at Jefferies in London. "What the Fed is doing, by trying to drive down the yield curve, draws a parallel with Japan. In Europe, inflation is low and deflation risk is facing many countries."\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Federal Reserve, citing concerns about growth and in a move widely read as a strike against potential deflation, said it would augment its quantitative-easing program by acquiring more bonds. The Bank of England on Wednesday downgraded its forecast and also kindled expectation it may take more extraordinary action to boost the U.K. economy, despite stubborn inflation.\par \par \uc2 These actions contrasted with the Bank of Japan's decision this week to stand pat, despite calls for action from the government and a still relatively weak economic recovery. The BOJ's stronger stance helped give the yen a push to trade as high as 84.72 to the dollar, its best level since July 1995, before retreating by early afternoon in New York to near its late-Tuesday level of 85.36 yen.\par \par \uc2 "Japanese authorities' response to yen gains remains slow," notes \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Tomoko Fujii. With Japan unwilling to match the Fed's aggressiveness at getting interest rates as low as possible, U.S. and Japanese interest rates are approaching each other, a phenomenon that should give the yen a relative boost.\par \par \uc2 The latest wild card driving yen strength has been China. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  superpower shifted a slice of its $2.5 trillion worth of reserves into yen this year, after years of eschewing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On Monday, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that this year through June, China had bought $20.2 billion worth of yen, almost five times what it had bought in the previous five years.\par \par \uc2 An economist at a Chinese government-backed think tank wrote Wednesday that Chinese interest in yen is based partly on the fact that Japanese government bonds are held mostly by local investors, making them safer than Treasuries, which are held by both domestic and foreign investors, including China. It wasn't clear whether his views reflect the thinking of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese government bond market is closed and the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate has been strong against the dollar in recent years," wrote Zhang Ming, economist at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences. "These are important reasons why Chinese investors increased their [Japanese government bond] holdings," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The strong yen hurts Japanese exporters and has dragged down the stock market there. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 2.7% to 9292.85, leaving it down nearly 12% this year, on worries about the strong yen and a report that Japanese machinery orders grew less than expected.\par \par \uc2 In response, Japan's Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry announced that it will conduct an emergency survey of approximately 200 companies to assess how the recently strong yen has affected their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nikkei business daily reported on its online edition.\par \par \uc2 Other factors adding to the yen's strength include the country's persistent current-account surplus, $12.3 billion in June, which is driven by a strong export sector. Japanese government limitations on the amount of leverage retail investors can use to invest in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  went into effect Aug. 1 and are seen as having contributed to the strong yen.\par \par \uc2 There may be little that Japan can do without the help of allies in the U.S. and Europe to send the yen lower. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- in which a country sells its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and purchases another's -- is expensive and difficult to pull off without a coordinated buying and selling effort.\par \par \uc2 "Japan likely couldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the consent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Takahide Kiuchi, chief economist at Nomura Securities.\par \par \uc2 It's unlikely that the U.S. would work with Japan on weakening the yen and strengthening the dollar. A weak dollar benefits American exporters, and the Obama administration has made it a goal to double U.S. exports over the next five years as way to rebalance economic growth away from consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is geopolitically less palatable than it may have been six years ago, the last time Japan bought dollars for yen on the open market. Japan has joined the chorus of nations that criticize China for its heavily managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which Western economists say keeps the Chinese yuan artificially low as a way to make China's exports more competitive. The U.S. hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since September 2000.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has shied away from the urgent rhetoric it used before previous yen-selling campaigns, the last of which it conducted in 2004. Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda has adjusted his tone only slightly over the past week in response to the yen's gains.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday evening in Tokyo, after the dollar dropped below 85 yen, Mr. Noda repeated his most recent statements, saying merely, "I am watching this development extremely closely."\par \par \uc2 If previous examples are any guide, an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be preceded with language such as threatening "appropriate action," or even more forebodingly, "bold and decisive action."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Noda did, however, call for the Bank of Japan to take more action that would mirror the Fed's moves. "With regard to problems such as the strong yen or deflation, we want to cooperate with the Bank of Japan more closely than ever before," Mr. Noda said.\par \par \uc2 As the yen rises, so does the political pressure to do something. On Tuesday, leader of the opposition Your Party, Masaaki Shirakawa, called for the Bank of Japan to begin a 20 trillion yen ($230 billion) quantitative-easing program, according to Reuters. "The yen is surging against the dollar and could damage our economy," he wrote in a letter to Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa.\par \par \uc2 But dealers say the long-term effectiveness of such measures could be limited. The yen did weaken in the weeks following the BOJ's December move to expand a lending facility, but it has tended to strengthen against the dollar with subsequent falls in U.S. interest rates, which Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't control.\par \par \uc2 Exactly how much a strong yen affects Japan's overall economy is widely debated. While it hurts exporters' profits and Japan's stock market generally, a strong yen also lowers costs for imported raw materials such as oil and parts used in Japan's high-tech manufacturing sector.\par \par \uc2 There's a growing sense of frustration in Japan that while the weak yen policy of the last decade benefited the export sector and some specific companies, it did little to shake Japan out of its 20 year struggle with falling overall prices.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Takashi Nakamichi, Megumi Fujikawa, Tomoyuki Tachikawa and Liu Li contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2010 14:42 ET (18:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100811e68b004qr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
