\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Global support shows euro zone rescue means business\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Dolan  \par \uc2 1,000 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2010\par 17:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 12 (Reuters) - The scale of global backing for last weekend's trillion dollar euro zone rescue \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  made it one of the most ambitious and aggressive market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the last thirty years.\par \par \uc2 The involvement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the economies of the Group of Seven and the Group of 20, the International Monetary Fund, the 16-nation euro group and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed this was not just another regional twist in a protracted crisis.\par \par \uc2 As Greece's debt crisis threatened to infect the rest of the euro area and go global, the response needed to be global too.\par \par \uc2 There was a line drawn in the sand, a commitment to do whatever it takes and plans to urgently remedy the root cause of the market mistrust -- budget cuts and better coordination.\par \par \uc2 "It's the most important and impressive policy response to a potential global crisis that I've seen since the Plaza Accord," said Jim O'Neill, Chief Global Economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , referring to 1985's successful pact between the United States, Japan, West Germany, Britain and France to weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar lost 50 percent against Japan's yen by 1987.\par \par \uc2 After Monday's initial market shock at a 750 billion euro EU/IMF package to underpin euro government debt markets and the ECB's unprecedented bond-buying spree, market players naturally searched for holes in the plan.\par \par \uc2 After the seven percent surge in European equities on Monday and up to a halving of debt market premia on blighted Greek, Portuguese and Spanish bonds paused and hesitated on Tuesday before resuming with more measured gains on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The euro's exchange remains lower than Friday's levels, although that was more to do with the effective easing of monetary conditions engineered by the ECB's bond buying than a judgement of the debt rescue package per se.\par \par \uc2 BONDS NOT \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 But even though Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at unruly bonds rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unlike most prior G7 market forays, it still bore all hallmarks of those agreements.\par \par \uc2 Yet as celebrated as the Plaza Accord has become, last weekend's action probably owes more to the international accord to support the ailing euro in September 2000; or the G7/G20 pledge not to allow other systemically important banks to go bust after \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 ' tumultuous demise in 2008.\par \par \uc2 "The euro \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  model of 2000 is exactly the right way to think about this," said Goldman's O'Neill.\par \par \uc2 In September 22 2000 central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted together for first time in five years and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the euro higher after it hit an all-time low below 85 cents two days earlier, a fall of nearly 30 percent since its January 1999 launch.\par \par \uc2 At the time the precipitous decline of the euro threatened to destabilise world markets and the United States, led by then Treasury Secretary and now White House adviser Lawrence Summers, was as keen to stabilise the situation as the Europeans and they acted the morning before a G7 finance ministers dinner in Prague.\par \par \uc2 After the initial shock, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets attempted to push the euro back lower within 48 hours, but the ECB continued its heavy and regular unilateral euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for at least six weeks afterward. The euro/dollar exchange rate was 50 percent higher within two years.\par \par \uc2 FIRST BASE\par \par \uc2 The immediate task this time was to cap prohibitively expensive borrowing rates to manageable levels for the most affected euro members and assuage growing investor unease about the risk of sovereign insolvency and default spreading across the world in the wake of Greece's messy and delayed bailout.\par \par \uc2 The alarm bells rang to mobilise the G7, the G20, the IMF and for extraordinary EU and ECB action when the crisis spilled over to interbank lending on fears of another wave of bank asset writedowns and insolvencies akin to the worst moments of the 2007/2008 credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 Given that G20 governments had already committed to whatever was necessary to prevent that banking crisis and recession becoming a global depression, it learned from previous mistakes. If no systemically important bank was going to be allowed to fail, then no sovereign would to be allowed to default either.\par \par \uc2 For the ECB, the decision to buy euro zone bonds was a controversial new departure but it did shift the goalposts for financial speculators who had become increasingly emboldened betting against member governments' bonds.\par \par \uc2 In this regard, its move mirrored the EU's decision in 1993 to counter a year of constant speculation against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held in narrow trading bands within its Exchange Rate Mechanism by exploding those band widths more than sixfold. The euro was successfully launched six years later.\par \par \uc2 "By implementing this bold programme the ECB has now eradicated the sovereign liquidity crisis and avoided the global economy returning to the dark days of end 2008," said Jacques Cailloux, European economist RBS. "This weekend's historic decision is a sign that the ECB continues to mature. It is now one of the "grown up" central banks in the world that can afford to take bold actions without losing credibility."\par \par \uc2 The battle will likely take more than couple of days or a couple of weeks. Investors will question the progress on fiscal reform of euro area institutions and promised austerity plans.\par \par \uc2 "A sustained solution probably lies with a combination of fiscal consolidation and debt restructuring coupled with monetary stimulus in the form of full-blown QE (quantitative easing. This would bring needed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness," said David Shairp, global strategist at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "The sense of crisis may have passed but the underlying problems remain."\par \par \uc2 But if history is any guide then the Brussels agreement of May 2010 may mark a turning point.\par \par \uc2 EUROPE-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|FUN|SXNA  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100512e65c001rm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
