\par\par \b \uc2 TOPWRAP 9-U.S. vows bank aid; aims to avoid nationalization\b0\par\par\uc2 911 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2009\par 23:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks slammed on fears rescue will fall short\par \par \uc2 * U.S. to launch bank rescue program Wednesday\par \par \uc2 * Government may boost Citi stake - source\par \par \uc2 * France pledges aid for mutual banks set for merger (For more on the financial crisis, double click on nCRISIS])\par \par \uc2 By Emily Kaiser\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Feb 23 (Reuters) - U.S. regulators promised on Monday to prop up struggling banks if needed and said lenders should remain in private hands, even as a source said Citigroup was in talks to give the government a greater stake.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, the French government said it was pumping extra cash into two mutually owned banks, and central European central banks took the unprecedented step of talking up the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average, which started higher on the Citigroup talk, touched an 11-year low on fears that the bank stabilization plan would not be enough to keep the economy from sliding into a deeper hole. U.S. government bond prices rose slightly and the dollar gained against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors scrounged for safety.\par \par \uc2 Amid worries that the United States may still have to nationalize some of its banks, the Treasury Department, Federal Reserve and three other federal agencies said they will start examining large firms' capital needs on Wednesday to determine whether a bigger buffer is warranted.\par \par \uc2 The money could come from the private sector or the government in the form of preferred shares that convert into common stock over time as needed to ensure banks have enough resources to withstand deepening credit losses.\par \par \uc2 "Because our economy functions better when financial institutions are well managed in the private sector, the strong presumption of the Capital Assistance Program is that banks should remain in private hands," the agencies said.\par \par \uc2 Healthy banks are vital to stemming recession. While some economists have argued that nationalizing weaker institutions would be the fastest way to revive lending, many investors -- particularly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- worry that government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have a chilling effect on business.\par \par \uc2 "If the government tells the bank that they need more capital, it's highly unlikely any 'private' source would step up due to the stigma," said Andrew Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BMO Capital Markets in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 "This means that the government's designation could signal a further death spiral for the bank's common stock shareholders," he said.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup, whose stock has been pounded by fears that the government may seize the bank and wipe out shareholders, was in talks to give the government a larger stake, a person familiar with the matter told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The idea under consideration would involve converting a big chunk of the $45 billion in preferred shares the government bought last year into common stock, putting as much as 40 percent of Citigroup into public hands.\par \par \uc2 The British government announced a similar move last month, saying it would convert preferred shares in Royal Bank of Scotland, which is expected to announce more restructuring this week.\par \par \uc2 France on Monday also reached out to its lenders, pledging up to 5 billion euros ($6.3 billion) in additional aid for Banque Populaire and Groupe Caisse d'Epargne.\par \par \uc2 The two mutual banks are expected to detail a merger this week and the new aid could give the government a stake of up to 20 percent in what is set to be France's second-biggest retail bank behind Credit Agricole.\par \par \uc2 LOSSES MOUNT\par \par \uc2 Banks around the world have already reported hundreds of billions of dollars in losses and write-downs as defaults spike on mortgages, credit cards, corporate debt and a host of other loans.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co, the second-largest U.S. bank and considered to be among the strongest, slashed its common stock dividend by 87 percent in a surprise move to preserve a "fortress" balance sheet against any worsening of conditions.\par \par \uc2 American International Group, once the world's largest insurer, may be coming back for its third round of U.S. government aid as losses and writedowns mount, according to a source and a CNBC report.\par \par \uc2 Dresdner Kleinwort economists think corporate bankruptcies worldwide will rise by at least 20 percent this year, after a 14 percent increase in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Governments have responded by stepping up spending and tax cuts to try to cushion the blow. Despite the spending spike, U.S. President Barack Obama pledged to halve the budget deficit in the next four years.\par \par \uc2 In the auto industry, one of the hardest hit by the credit contraction and consumer spending slump, Ford reached a tentative agreement with the United Auto Workers union on changes to a retiree health care trust, becoming the first Detroit automaker to secure union concessions on the key issue.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Monday that the financial crisis was spilling over into the wider economy and that the euro zone's financial system is under "severe strain."\par \par \uc2 Latvia's government collapsed on Friday and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries such as Poland, the Czech Republic and Hungary have come under severe pressure, hitting millions of citizens who have borrowed in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro.\par \par \uc2 Emerging European Union central banks coordinated to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, with Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Zdenek Tuma saying they had agreed that recent falls were overplayed.\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/ (TOPWRAP 9)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090223e52n001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
