\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thai c.bank sees little impact from US, Europe woes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Trisanat Kongkhunthian   \par \uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2008\par 06:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, baht \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The financial crisis in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe is having little impact on Thailand but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to provide liquidity if the need arises, Bank of Thailand Governor Tarisa Watanagase said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The crisis is having little impact on Thailand, but we can't be complacent and have to keep monitoring," Tarisa told reporters after a meeting with economic ministers to discuss the impact.\par \par \uc2 "We are ready to help banks with liquidity if needed," she said.\par \par \uc2 She said Thai financial institutions had lent only 177 million baht ($5.2 million) to Benelux financial group Fortis and had about 2.5 billion baht in other outstanding obligations, which she did not specify.\par \par \uc2 Fortis has become the first major victim of the credit crisis in the euro zone and is being partially nationalised.\par \par \uc2 Tarisa has previously said Thai banks had exposure of about 7 billion baht in U.S. investment bank \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 , which filed for backruptcy protection this month.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 She said there were no unusual capital outflows despite a sell-off in Thai stocks after the global markets plunged.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday to keep the baht's moves in line with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she added.\par \par \uc2 "We are taking care of the baht as usual to keep it in line with the region, she said.\par \par \uc2 Tarisa did not say what action the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had taken, but traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought the baht to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening too quickly.\par \par \uc2 "If countries from the U.S. and Europe sell stocks and take their money home, our reserves are enough to cope with that," she said, referring to the country's foreign reserves, which stood at $101.5 billion on Sept. 19.\par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=TH> was around 34.01/04 per dollar at 0553 GMT, little changed from 34.04 at Monday's close.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Deputy Prime Minister Olarn Chaipravat told reporters the government saw no need to set up any special state-run funds to shore up the battered stock market, hit by the global financial turmoil.\par \par \uc2 "There is no need to set up any funds. The private sector and listed firms are still strong and liquidity is still good," he said.\par \par \uc2 Thai stocks <.SETI> opened down 5.51 percent on Tuesday, tracking the global market trend following the shock rejection by U.S. lawmakers of a $700 billion bailout plan for the U.S. financial sector.\par \par \uc2 The Thai market later recovered a little, narrowing the loss to 2.9 percent by the midsession break. (Writing by Orathai Sriring; Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/CRISIS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|ABX|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080930e49u0006z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
