\par\par \b \uc2 COLUMN-Time is now for  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to prop dollar: James Saft\b0\par\par\uc2 971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2008\par 21:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 -- James Saft is a Reuters columnist. The opinions expressed are his own --\par \par \uc2 By James Saft\par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 17 (Reuters) - The time may have come for sustained coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by governments to support the wilting U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has taken another leg downward on fear surrounding the bailout of \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  and the perilous state of banking in the United States, briefly breaching $1.60 against the euro and now down almost 7 percent this year against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. Federal Reserve, undertaken in concert with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other global economic powers, could be an inflexion point for the dollar after its 6 year fall.\par \par \uc2 And with the falling dollar playing a substantial role in rising oil prices, official action to back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could provide relief for consumers and ease the pressure from inflation, both in the United States and globally.\par \par \uc2 It would also be a very useful and timely insurance policy against any run on the dollar should global holders of U.S. debt take fright at what may be a massive bill, and proportionally huge supply of new U.S. debt, to backstop Fannie and Freddie and sort out problems in U.S. real estate and banking.\par \par \uc2 "This is a situation crying out for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and leadership," said Nick Parsons, head of market strategy at nabCapital in London.\par \par \uc2 "The benefit to (U.S.) exporters has been more than outweighed by the costs to consumers of a weak dollar."\par \par \uc2 The falling dollar is a problem -- and not just for the United States -- because it is associated with the rising cost of energy, which in turn feeds inflation and chokes off consumption.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chairman Bernanke acknowledged both the problem and the link with oil in June, when in highly unusual comments he said he was "attentive" to the inflationary impact of a sinking dollar, combining this with tough talk about inflation that led investors to bet U.S. rates were heading upward.\par \par \uc2 A study by the Fed said that about a third of the rise in the price of oil between 2003 and 2007 was caused by a falling dollar.\par \par \uc2 Bernanke on Wednesday told Congress there "may be conditions in which markets are disorderly where some temporary action may be justified."\par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary Hank Paulson has been doing his bit too, saying time after time that a strong dollar, set by free markets, is in the best interests of the United States and the world.\par \par \uc2 Problem is that markets have called Bernanke's bluff and now realise that, with finance in an abject state, he's in no position to raise rates to support the dollar and choke off inflation.\par \par \uc2 As for Paulson, the market long ago concluded that the strong dollar mantra is a meaningless piety, though he did say in June he'd never take \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "off the table".\par \par \uc2 Bernanke on Wednesday told Congress there "may be conditions in which markets are disorderly where some temporary action may be justified." A pattern of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments from both sides of the Atlantic is probably a precondition for actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STARS COMING INTO ALIGNMENT?\par \par \uc2 Talking the Europeans into it would not prove too hard. The European economy is suffering under the burden of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with industrial output falling by the most in 16 years in May. And though a falling euro would import inflation, this must be set off against the prospect of lower energy prices and the relief they would bring.\par \par \uc2 For the United States, the biggest obstacles are probably ideological. The Bush administration has long argued that the value of the dollar should be set in the free and unfettered market, though where exactly a free market can be found I am increasingly unclear.\par \par \uc2 The serial bailouts, first of \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2  and now \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 , have robbed that argument of much of its moral and intellectual authority, though probably not all of its emotional appeal.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the dollar up would have costs; it would be unpopular with exporters, one of the few parts of the economy that is doing well. The benefits for consumers could be both big and easy to sell in an election year, however.\par \par \uc2 The United States must also be weighing the possibility that international support for its debt fades as the crisis in its financial sector worsens.\par \par \uc2 The cost of buying insurance against a default by the United States has risen markedly, to a still tiny 18-20 basis points from its usual two or less.\par \par \uc2 With neither the costs or the scope of the emerging bailout known, it is possible that foreign investors begin to impose a markedly higher risk on U.S. government assets. Remember too that the recapitalisation of the U.S. financial system depends in part on attracting a fair amount of new risk capital from overseas, and investors may decide that buying into a very weak industry in a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not attractive.\par \par \uc2 No one can argue that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will change the very poor fundamentals in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Savings still have to rise, housing prices still have to fall and standards of living will fall for a time.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can provide some relief.\par \par \uc2 No one wants to start from here, but here is where we find ourselves.\par \par \uc2 -- At the time of publication James Saft did not own any direct investments in securities mentioned in this article. He may be an owner indirectly as an investor in a fund. You can email him at jsaft@reuters.com and find more columns at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/columns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/columns}}}\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-COLUMN/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080720e47h000g7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
