\par\par \b \uc2 CHRONOLOGY-Milestones in euro's history; $1.6038 peak\b0\par\par\uc2 1,276 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2008\par 13:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 15 (Reuters) - The euro rose to a record high of $1.6038 on Tuesday, as the U.S. dollar sank broadly on deepening concern about the U.S. financial system and less than two weeks after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The euro's return above $1.60 -- for the first time in three months -- represents a near doubling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value from its record low just above 82 cents in October 2000.\par \par \uc2 * July 15, 2008 - Euro sets record high at $1.6038 after U.S. government support for state-sponsored mortgage giants \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  two days earlier fails to quell concerns about wider U.S. financial stability. Move comes 12 days after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted interest rates by a quarter point to 4.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 * April 22, 2008 - Euro scales $1.60 for the first time to trade as high as $1.6018, according to Reuters data, representing a near doubling in its value against the dollar from its low of $0.8225 struck in October, 2000.\par \par \uc2 * March 12, 2008 - Euro rises above $1.55 after stronger-than-expected euro zone industrial production data bolstered the view the ECB will resist from following the U.S. Federal Reserve's lead in cutting interest rates to protect the economy from the ongoing credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 * Feb 27, 2008 - Euro trades above the key psychological $1.50 barrier, after Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke signalled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to cut rates again in the face of mounting risks to economic growth. The Fed had already slashed its key Fed funds target rate by 225 basis points to 3 percent since mid-September in a bid to stem the economic impact form the global credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 * Oct 31, 2007 - Euro climbs above $1.45, for the first time ever, on the day the U.S. Federal Reserve ended a two-day policy meeting with a 25 basis point cut in its key interest rate to 4.50 percent. The Fed's cut was smaller than many analysts had expected, but fuelled expectations of more easing in the meeting's ahead and narrowed further the dollar's yield advantage over the euro to only half a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 * Oct 18, 2007 - Euro climbs above $1.43 for the first time ever to as high as $1.4310, according to Reuters data, as the dollar sags across the board on the back of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 's earnings and weekly U.S. jobless claims data.\par \par \uc2 * Oct 1, 2007 - Euro prints a fresh all-time high of $1.4281, according to Reuters data, after tame U.S. inflation data fans expectations the Fed will soon deliver another rate cut.\par \par \uc2 * Sept 20, 2007 - Euro breaks above the psychological $1.40 level for the first time, continuing its rally since the Fed slashed its fed funds and discount rates by 50 basis points to 4.75 percent and 5.25 percent, respectively, on Sept 18 in a bid to contain the economic damage from the unfolding credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 * April 27, 2007 - Euro rises to $1.3682, according to Reuters data, past its previous peak around $1.3670 from Dec. 2004, some 17 percent above its launch level in 1999 and 66 percent above the record low set in October 2000.\par \par \uc2 * Nov, 2006 - Euro breaks back above $1.30 for first time in 18 months as signs of a strong German economic recovery boosts ECB rate rise expectations, and continued talk of central banks diversifying reserves out of the dollar weighs on the greenback.\par \par \uc2 * Dec 1, 2005 - ECB raises key rates by a quarter percentage point from record low of 2 percent, the first in a series of hikes that takes rates as high as 3.75 percent by March 2007. Euro closes at $1.1732.\par \par \uc2 * Dec 30, 2004 - Euro trades at $1.3670, according to EBS data, after the dollar is hit by a wave of concern about the ballooning U.S. current account deficit. The soaring euro prompts ECB chief Trichet to warn markets that "brutal" moves in exchange rates were unwelcome.\par \par \uc2 * June 30, 2004 - Fed raises rates by a quarter percentage point from record low of 1 percent, the first in a series of hikes that take rates as high as 5.25 percent by June 2006. Euro closes at $1.2185.\par \par \uc2 * Feb 7, 2004 - G7 finance chiefs meeting in Boca Raton, Florida warn against "excess volatility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Euro closes at $1.2688 on Feb 9.\par \par \uc2 * Nov 1, 2003 - Jean-Claude Trichet takes over from Wim Duisenberg as ECB president. Euro trades at $1.1582.\par \par \uc2 * Sept 20, 2003 - G7 finance chiefs meeting in Dubai call for "more flexibility" in global exchange rates to ease growing international imbalances -- a reference seen to apply mainly to China but also interpreted as a broader sign G7 wants to see a weaker dollar help redress global imbalances. The euro closes at $1.1465 on Sept 22, having traded as low as $1.0760 earlier in the month.\par \par \uc2 * June 5, 2003 - ECB cuts key rates by 50 basis points to record low of 2 percent. Euro closes at $1.1697.\par \par \uc2 * March 20, 2003 - U.S.-led forces invade Iraq. Euro closes at $1.0531 on March 21.\par \par \uc2 * June 28, 2002 - Euro rises back above $1.00. Pressure on the dollar intensifies as a two-year-old bear market in U.S. equity markets deepens following months of concern about corporate accounting scandals from Enron to WorldCom.\par \par \uc2 * Jan 2, 2002 - Euro gains one percent to rise over $0.90 in a wave of enthusiasm inspired by the smooth introduction of euro notes and coins in the then 12 countries of the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 * Sept 11, 2001 - Two hijacked planes hit twin towers of New York's World Trade Center. Euro rises two percent to $0.9169.\par \par \uc2 * May 10, 2001 - ECB cuts key rates by a quarter percentage point to 4.5 percent, the first in a series of 7 cuts that takes rates down to a record low of 2 percent by June 2003. Euro closes at $0.8812.\par \par \uc2 * Jan 3, 2001 - U.S. Federal Reserve cuts the federal funds rate 50 basis points to 6.0 percent. The move marks start of aggressive interest rate cuts which take rates as low as 1 percent by June 2003. Euro closes at $0.9281.\par \par \uc2 * Nov 10, Nov 6, Nov 3, 2000 - ECB in conjunction with national euro zone central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros, leaving the euro trading around $0.86.\par \par \uc2 * Oct 26, 2000 - Euro hits record low of $0.8225, 30 percent below its launch value.\par \par \uc2 * Oct 3, 2000 - U.S. tech stock "bubble" peaks as Nasdaq sets record high. Euro closes at $0.8752.\par \par \uc2 * Sept 22, 2000 - Central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting together for first time since 1995, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive euro higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits low below $0.85 two days earlier. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bout is first by new ECB.\par \par \uc2 * Dec 2, 1999 - Euro falls to one-for-one with the dollar for the first time. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg says euro has strong potential to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 * Apr 8, 1999 - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes its first monetary policy move, lowering key refinancing rate 50 basis points to 2.50 percent. Euro closes at $1.0798.\par \par \uc2 * Jan 4, 1999 - Euro begins trading at $1.1747, hitting a high of $1.1906 on the same day.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Victoria Main)\par 