\par\par \b \uc2 CHRONOLOGY-Milestones in the ECB's history\b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2008\par 15:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 May 23 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  celebrates its 10-year anniversary on June 1. Following is a list of the key points in the history of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . * June 1, 2008 - The ECB celebrates its 10th anniversary * May 7, 2008 - The European Commission judges that Slovakia meets the criteria to become the 16th country to adopt the euro on Jan. 1, 2009 * April 22, 2008 - The euro hits an all-time high of $1.6018, almost double its value compared to the low of $0.8225 struck in October 2000. * March 2008 - Euro zone inflation hits a record high of 3.6 percent. 1999 was the only calendar year since the euro's launch when inflation averaged below 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 * Jan. 1, 2008 - Cyprus and Malta become the 14th and 15th countries to adopt the euro * Dec. 12, 2007 - The ECB and the world's other major central central banks announce joint action to stabilise money markets still roiled by the U.S. subprime crisis, for the first time since the aftermath of the Sept. 11 attacks * Aug. 9, 2007 - The ECB injects 94.8 billion euros ($130.6 billion) in overnight funds into the euro zone money market, in the first of an ongoing series of actions to stabilise interbank lending markets hit by spillover from the U.S. subprime crisis\par \par \uc2 * June 6, 2007 - The ECB raises its main interest rate to 4 percent from 3.75 percent, where it subsequently plateaus\par \par \uc2 * Jan. 1, 2007 - Slovenia adopts the euro as its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , becoming the first of the 10 mostly east European countries that joine the EU in 2004 to do so * Dec. 1, 2005 - ECB raises its main interest rate for the first time in two-and-a-half years, starting a series of quarter point hikes that takes rates to 4.0 percent in June 2007.\par \par \uc2 * Nov. 1, 2003 - Jean-Claude Trichet takes over from Wim Duisenberg as ECB president. * Sept. 14, 2003 - Sweden rejects joining the euro zone in a referendum * June 5, 2003 - The ECB lowers its main interest rate to 2 percent, a record low where it remains until Dec. 1, 2005. * May 8, 2003 - The ECB redefines its medium-term price stability target as being inflation 'below but close to' 2 percent, after earlier being simply 'below' 2 percent * Jan. 1, 2002 - Euro notes and coins are introduced smoothly across the 12 countries in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 * Sept. 13, 2001 - ECB and Fed agree \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap agreements to stabilise financial markets after the Sept. 11 attacks on the United States\par \par \uc2 * May 10, 2001 - ECB cuts key rates by a quarter percentage point to 4.5 percent, the start of a cycle of 7 cuts that takes rates down to a record low of 2 percent by June 2003. * Jan. 1, 2001 - Greece becomes the 12th country to adopt the euro\par \par \uc2 * November 2000 - ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times to buy euros, leaving the euro trading around $0.86.\par \par \uc2 * Oct. 26, 2000 - Euro hits record low of $0.8225, 30 percent below its launch value as U.S. stock markets boom. * Oct. 5, 2000 - ECB raises rates to 4.75 percent from 4.50 percent, the highest rate in the ECB's history\par \par \uc2 * Sept. 28, 2000 - Denmark rejects joining the euro in a referendum * Sept. 22, 2000 - Central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  act together for first time since 1995, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and drive euro higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls below $0.85. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bout is first by new ECB.\par \par \uc2 * Nov. 4, 1999 - The ECB raises rates to 3 percent from 2.5 percent, starting a rate-tightening cycle that peaks at 4.75 percnent on Oct. 5, 2000 * April 8, 1999 - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes its first monetary policy move, lowering the key refinancing rate 50 basis points to 2.50 percent.\par \par \uc2 * Jan. 4, 1999 - The euro begins trading at $1.1747 * Jan. 1, 1999 - The euro is introduced as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 11 countries -- Austria, Belgium, Finland, France, Germany, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Portugal and Spain, -- with a benchmark interest rate of 3 percent * June 1, 1998 - The ECB is established in Frankfurt with the Netherlands' Wim Duisenberg as president, taking over final preparations for the euro's launch from the European Monetary Institute * December 1995 - EU countries pick the name 'euro' for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and set a Jan. 1, 1999 start date\par \par \uc2 * December 1991 - European Union countries meeting in Maastricht in the Netherlands agree a framework for setting up a single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Treaty on European Union\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by David Milliken and Marc Jones)\par \par \uc2 ECB-ANNIVERSARY/CHRONOLOGY|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080523e45n001h6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
