\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-IMF chief says global outlook dims, to cut forecasts   \b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 12:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 PARIS, March 17 (Reuters) - The global economic outlook is worsening and the risk of contagion from a financial markets crisis that began in the United States is now very high, IMF chief Dominique Strauss-Kahn said on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 At a news conference in Paris, he welcomed the overnight steps taken by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said the International Monetary Fund would be cutting its growth forecasts again soon.   \par \par \uc2 He also said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve were managing the market liquidity troubles very well, and that the situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while tricky, did not in his view call for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Obviously the financial markets crisis which started in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now more serious and even more global than it was a few weeks ago. The risks of contagion are very high," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Regarding the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest decisions on interest rates and lending facilities and involvement in the  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  takeover of  \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2 , he said:   \par \par \uc2 "I think I can just welcome the decisive and prompt measures taken by the U.S. Fed...What has been done by the Fed comes in due time, and the rest of which could be done by central banks will certainly be in line."   \par \par \uc2 "The central banks, the Fed, the ECB, others, have in my opinion very well managed the question of the lack of liquidity. So there's no reason to believe that they won't be able to deal with the liquidity question in the coming weeks."   \par \par \uc2 The Fed cut the discount rate to 3.25 percent on Sunday.   \par \par \uc2 The yuan and yen looked weak, the euro overvalued, and the dollar somewhere in-between, said Strauss-Kahn, holding a news conference he held jointly with OECD chief Angel Gurria.   \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is indeed starting to weaken, it's true, but the weakest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the dollar," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "The weakest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are Chinese renminbi and Japanese yen in part," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "The whole monetary system is starting to become stretched now, with, on the strong side, the euro, and clearly on the weak side the Chinese and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and between the two the dollar," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "For a long time the dollar was in a situation where its downward movement was predictable. We are now in a situation that is more stretched and this more stretched situation is not a situation now in which it seems to me that central banks need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The current climate was not a problem for depositors.   \par \par \uc2 "It's a problem for economic growth. We clearly face a situation in which the risks to economic growth are clearly more and more serious." The IMF would be lowering its economic growth forecasts in the coming weeks, including its forecast for the United States, Europe and China, he said.   \par \par \uc2 The OECD's Gurria said more public money might be needed to sort out the problems and more monetary easing might be needed, notably in the United States.   \par \par \uc2 Both men said their institutions would be lowering their economic forecasts, in coming days in the OECD's case and the coming weeks in the IMF's case.   \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-IMF/STRAUSSKAHN (