\par\par \b \uc2 New Zealand/Australia Morning Call-Global markets   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 19:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2   -----------------------(07:18 / 1818\par GMT)---------------------- Stock Markets\par  S&P/ASX 200    5,380.30  -25.50  NZSX 50        3,584.47 \par +0.21\par  DJIA          12,091.68 -167.22  Nikkei        12,992.28 \par +0.10\par  NASDAQ         2,233.26  -25.34  FTSE           5,767.70\par -50.90\par  S&P 500        1,314.91  -16.43  Hang Seng     23,584.97\par -465.10\par  SPI 200 Fut    5,340.00  -76.00  CRB Index        410.60 \par -6.14\par  Bonds                                                        \par   AU 10 YR Bond    93.880  +0.050  US 10 YR Bond     3.522\par -0.030\par  NZ 10 YR Bond     6.900  +0.005  US 30 YR Bond     4.436\par -0.001\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Prev at 5pm AEST)                                \par   AUD US$          0.9245  0.9294  NZD US$          0.7977\par +0.8021\par  EUR US$          1.5222  1.5183  Yen US$          102.83\par +103.45\par  Commodities                                                  \par   Gold (Lon)       984.75          Silver (Lon)     20.320    \par Gold (NY)      #N/A N/A          Light Crude      100.25   \par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Overnight market action. An updated report will be sent after\par the close of New York markets.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  EQUITIES   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  NEW YORK - U.S. stocks dropped more than 1 percent on\par Tuesday as concerns about further losses at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  weighed\par on the financial sector and a sharp pullback in oil prices\par dragged down energy shares.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  A  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst forecast wider losses at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\par Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the largest U.S. bank, and its stock dropped 6.2\par percent to $21.66. In addition, the Dubai investment agency\par said  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  may need more capital from outside investors.\par [ID:nWNA5081] [ID:nL041008].   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> was down 146.78\par points, or 1.19 percent, at 12,112.12. The Standard & Poor's\par 500 Index <.SPX> was down 15.89 points, or 1.19 percent, at\par 1,315.45. The Nasdaq Composite Index <.IXIC> was down 25.23\par points, or 1.12 percent, at 2,233.37.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  In the tech world,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Intel Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the world's largest\par chip maker, said late Monday that weaker prices for some of its\par memory chips could hurt profits and lowered its current-quarter\par gross margin forecast, increasing worries about business\par spending. For details, see [ID:nN03407754].   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  LONDON - Britain's FTSE 100 <.FTSE> index fell for the\par fifth straight session to close down 0.9 percent on Tuesday,\par led by banks and oil shares, but fund manager Schroders \par shone after its 2007 results beat expectations.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The FTSE 100 ended down 50.9 points at 5,767.7, hitting a\par three-week closing low and following a 1.1-percent loss on\par Monday as investors fretted about a looming U.S. recession.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The UK benchmark index has lost more than 10 percent so far\par this year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Banks wiped 17 points off the index on Tuesday as concerns\par mounted over possible trading losses and banks' ability to lend\par to hedge funds if the credit crunch continues.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  TOKYO - Japan's Nikkei share average ended flat on Tuesday,\par with banks such as  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par falling on worries about the economic outlook, offsetting gains\par in resource-related shares amid rising commodity prices.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Consumer electronics maker  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pioneer Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2   jumped\par more than 11 percent on news it is planning to restructure its\par loss-making TV \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The benchmark index gyrated between positive and negative\par ground throughout the session, failing to stay in either\par territory for a sustained period of time.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The benchmark Nikkei average <.N225> ended flat, up just\par 0.1 point at 12,992.28. The broader TOPIX index <.TOPX> lost\par 0.4 percent to 1,265.66.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2  NEW YORK - The dollar fell versus the yen and Swiss franc\par on Tuesday after Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke gave a\par grim assessment of the U.S. housing sector, adding to mounting\par fears of a recession in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by euro finance ministers ahead of the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting on Thursday halted the euro's\par five-day run of record highs versus the dollar.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  However, the euro maintained its upward bias against the\par dollar, with investors convinced that the poor U.S. economic\par outlook will keep the Fed on its easing path.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The dollar dipped to a session low of 102.66 yen <JPY=>,\par within striking distance of a three-year trough of 102.62 yen\par touched on Monday, according to Reuters data. It was last\par trading at 102.70 yen, down 0.5 percent on the day.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The euro fell 0.4 percent to 156.36 yen <EURJPY=> and\par dropped 0.6 percent to 1.5751 Swiss francs <EURCHF=>. Against\par the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar declined 0.6 percent to 1.0354\par Swiss francs <CHF=>.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  TREASURIES   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  NEW YORK - U.S. Treasury debt prices rose in choppy trading\par on Tuesday on renewed safe-haven bids spurred by a stock market\par sell-off and credit worries led by speculation of widening\par losses at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  An eagerly awaited speech by Federal Reserve Chairman Ben\par Bernanke shed no fresh insight into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's intent\par on future interest rate cuts, but his view of a prolonged\par housing slump supported the prevalent notion that the Fed would\par ease monetary policy further to stave off a U.S. recession.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  This bleak outlook, together with expectations of more Fed\par rate cuts, triggered a fresh wave of curve-steepening trades\par which involve purchases of short maturity Treasuries and sales\par of longer-dated issues, traders and analysts said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The yield gap between two-year and 10-year Treasuries\par briefly hit 195 basis points, its widest since July 2004.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Two-year yields <US2YT=RR> fell 4 basis points to 1.60\par percent. The price on the two-year note, which moves inversely\par with yield, was up 2/32 at 100-25/32.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Ten-year yields <US10YT=RR> dipped 1 basis point to 3.54\par percent. The price on the 10-year note was up 3/32 at 99-21/32.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  COMMODITIES   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  GOLD   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  NEW YORK - U.S. gold futures along with other precious\par metals suddenly reversed course with crude oil prices on\par Tuesday, falling sharply after platinum and palladium set new\par record and contract highs, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Gold futures for April delivery <GCJ8> on the COMEX\par division of the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped $19.90 to\par $964.50 an ounce after reaching an earlier high at $990.30 and\par off the record high at $992.00 reached Monday morning.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Spot gold <XAU=> was quoted at $964.60/965.50, down from\par $981.20/982.00 at the close on Monday. London bullion dealers\par fixed the afternoon spot price at $984.75 an ounce.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  COMEX's May silver <SIK8> lost 31.5 cents, or 1.56 percent,\par to $19.8650 an ounce, after surging to a 28-year high of\par $20.740 on Monday. It fell as low as $19.7550 an ounce.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  BASE METALS   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  LONDON - Industrial metals turned lower at the close on the\par London Metal Exchange on Tuesday as investors took profits and\par most commodity markets experienced a sell-off, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Commodities including metals have undergone dizzying\par rallies this year, with platinum up 40 percent and soft\par commodities as much 70 percent. Copper and aluminium are up by\par over 25 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Copper for delivery in three months <MCU3> fell 2 percent\par or $169 to $8,410 per tonne, having earlier rallied above\par $8,630, just below the previous day's 22-month high of $8,661.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Tin <MSN3> set a fresh contract high of $19,375 per tonne\par on concerns about supply as the market fretted over a renewed\par government crackdown on illegal tin mining in major supplier\par Indonesia. It ended at $19,050, up $100 from Monday's close.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Aluminium <MAL3> shed $23 to $3,115 per tonne, zinc <MZN3>\par was down $45 at $2,780. Lead <MPB3> fell $70 to $3,365 per\par tonne and nickel <MNI3> was $405 lower at $32,845.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  OIL   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  NEW YORK - U.S. crude oil futures slumped more than $3\par Tuesday, backing further off the record high hit a day earlier,\par as U.S. stocks tumbled on Wall Street and analysts forecast\par another increase in domestic crude stocks, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  On the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  at 12 noon EST (1600\par GMT), April crude <CLJ8> was down $2.11 or 2.06 percent at\par $100.34 a barrel, trading from $99.30 to $103.33 a barrel.\par Monday's surge took prices to a record $103.95.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  In London, April Brent crude <LCOJ8> slid $2.30 or 2.3\par percent at $100.48 a barrel, moving from $97.45 to $101.31.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Earlier, crude futures were up slightly, with the dollar's\par recent weakness and international tensions remaining supportive\par after sending crude prices to a record near $104 on Monday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  - - - -\par \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-NEWZEALAND-GLOBAL|LANGEN|Table|ND|AUF|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080304e434001j0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
