\par\par \b \uc2 Interest rate spread could hit Canadian businesses   \b0\par\par\uc2 By John McCrank   \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2008\par 22:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO, Jan 22 (Reuters) - Life could soon get a little tougher for some Canadian businesses, as the differential between interest rates in Canada and the United States widened on Tuesday and could grow even more by the end of next week.   \par \par \uc2 In an emergency move, the U.S. Federal Reserve slashed its key lending rate by 75 basis points, to 3.50 percent. That was followed by a milder 25 basis point cut by the  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 , in a scheduled announcement, putting its overnight rate at 4.00 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates, compared with the United States, will give support to the Canadian dollar, which has already been causing pain for manufacturers and exporters. Borrowing costs will also be higher in Canada on a relative basis.   \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed that 12 of 17 U.S. primary security dealers expect another cut of 50 basis points from the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Jan. 30, its regularly scheduled meeting day.   \par \par \uc2 "I think the market is prepared for a 50 (point cut) from the Fed at the end of the month and that would further emphasize the disparity between the two central banks and create a slightly different borrowing environment," said Eric Lascelles, chief economics and rates strategist at TD Securities.   \par \par \uc2 If the Fed cut goes ahead, that would take the spread between interest rates to a full percentage point, at least until the  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  next sets monetary policy in March.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts are calling for a 50 basis point cut by the  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  in March but, beyond that, the bank's hands may be tied.   \par \par \uc2 "The BoC is quick to mention that the economy currently continues to operate above its production capacity so the scope for further rate cuts remains somewhat limited by this fact," said Stewart Hall, market Strategist at  \uc2 HSBC Canada.\par \par \uc2 C$ STRENGTH, EXPORT WEAKNESS   \par \par \uc2 A differential of one percentage point in North American interest rates would not be unprecedented. That was the case as recently as 2006, but some Canadian businesses are still reeling from the rapid rise of the Canadian dollar, and more pain may be in store.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose a hefty 17.5 percent against the U.S. dollar in 2007, walloping Canadian manufacturers and exporters who found it increasingly hard to compete in the U.S. market.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys around 75 percent of Canadian exports.   \par \par \uc2 Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, along with a 25 basis point cut to the overnight rate in December, and some soft domestic data, helped sap some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.   \par \par \uc2 By Monday, the Canadian dollar had fallen over 12 percent from its modern-day high of US$1.1039, hit in November, and was around 97 U.S. cents on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 But the Canadian dollar rose after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcements and more strength may be in store.   \par \par \uc2 George Davis, chief technical strategist at  \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  said in a note that a close above 97.32 U.S. cents by the Canadian dollar would point toward a reversal in the recent trend to softness.   \par \par \uc2 The next short-term target for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be 99.37 U.S. cents, he said.   \par \par \uc2 ($1=$1.03 Canadian) (Editing by Rob Wilson)   \par