\par\par \b \uc2 ECB's Trichet says "brutal"  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  moves never welcome   \b0\par\par\uc2 273 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2007\par 15:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT, Nov 8 (Reuters) - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday "brutal" exchange rate moves were never good for the world economy, as the dollar teetered close to all-time lows against the euro again.   \par \par \uc2 "I have said already, brutal moves are never welcome," Trichet told a news conference after the ECB left interest rates on hold at 4 percent for the fifth month running.   \par \par \uc2 The euro has hit record highs against the dollar <EUR=> and major trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <EUREER=ECBF> as traders exploit the narrowing interest rate gap with the United States and concerns about U.S. growth in the wake of the subprime mortgage crisis.   \par \par \uc2 By 1248 GMT, the euro was steady on the day at $1.4653 <EUR=>. In January 2004, Trichet used the word "brutal" to describe a sharp rise in the euro, then at record highs of $1.2898.   \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the ECB chief said he had seen "sharp and abrupt" moves in exchange rates, adding that the Group of Seven finance ministers' position on the undesirability of disorderly movements was true more than ever.   \par \par \uc2 He also emphasised the U.S. government's stated position that it is in favour of a strong dollar. "It is even clearer that a strong dollar is in the interests of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The ECB last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in late 2000, to support the then weak euro. (Reporting by Krista Hughes, writing by Mike Peacock; Editing by Gerrard Raven)  