\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX - Euro facts and figures since 1999 launch   \b0\par\par\uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2007\par 09:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Sept 20 (Reuters) - The euro rose above $1.40 for the first time ever on Thursday, two days after the U.S. Federal Reserve aggressively cut its key fed funds rate and its discount rate.   \par \par \uc2 Following is a snapshot of the euro's performance against the U.S. dollar since its launch in January 1999:   \par \par \uc2 * Sept 20, 2007 - Euro breaks above the psychological $1.40 level for the first time, continuing its rally since the Fed slashed its fed funds and discount rates by 50 basis points to 4.75 percent and 5.25 percent, respectively, on Sept 18.   \par \par \uc2 * April 27, 2007 - Euro rises to $1.3682, according to Reuters data, past its previous peak around $1.3670 from Dec. 2004, some 17 percent above its launch level in 1999 and 66 percent above the record low set in October 2000.   \par \par \uc2 * Nov, 2006 - Euro breaks back above $1.30 for first time in 18 months as signs of a strong German economic recovery boosts European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate rise expectations, and continued talk of central banks diversifying reserves out of the dollar weighs on the greenback.   \par \par \uc2 * Dec 1, 2005 - ECB raises key rates by a quarter percentage point from record low of 2.0 percent, the first in a series of increases that take rates to 3.75 percent by March 2007. Euro closes at $1.1732.   \par \par \uc2 * Dec 30, 2004 - Euro trades at $1.3670, according to EBS data, after the dollar is hit by a wave of concern about the ballooning U.S. current account deficit. The soaring euro prompts ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet to warn markets that "brutal" moves in exchange rates were unwelcome.   \par \par \uc2 * June 30, 2004 - Fed raises rates by a quarter percentage point from record low of 1.0 percent, the first in a series of increases that take rates as high as 5.25 percent by June 2006. Euro closes at $1.2185.   \par \par \uc2 * Feb 7, 2004 - G7 finance chiefs meeting in Boca Raton, Florida warn against "excess volatility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Euro closes at $1.2688 on Feb 9.   \par \par \uc2 * Nov 1, 2003 - Jean-Claude Trichet takes over from Wim Duisenberg as ECB president. Euro trades at $1.1582.   \par \par \uc2 * Sept 20, 2003 - G7 finance chiefs meeting in Dubai call for "more flexibility" in global exchange rates to ease growing international imbalances -- a reference seen to apply mainly to China but also interpreted as a broader sign G7 wants to see a weaker dollar help redress global imbalances. The euro closes at $1.1465 on Sept 22, having traded as low as $1.0760 earlier in the month.   \par \par \uc2 * June 5, 2003 - ECB cuts key rates by 50 basis points to record low of 2.0 percent. Euro closes at $1.1697.   \par \par \uc2 * March 20, 2003 - U.S.-led forces invade Iraq. Euro closes at $1.0531 on March 21.   \par \par \uc2 * June 28, 2002 - Euro rises back above $1.00. Pressure on the dollar intensifies as a two-year-old bear market in U.S. equity markets deepens following months of concern about corporate accounting scandals from Enron to WorldCom.   \par \par \uc2 * Jan 2, 2002 - Euro gains one percent to rise over $0.90 in a wave of enthusiasm inspired by the smooth introduction of euro notes and coins in the then 12 countries of the euro zone.   \par \par \uc2 * Sept 11, 2001 - Two hijacked planes hit twin towers of New York's World Trade Center. Euro rises two percent to $0.9169.   \par \par \uc2 * May 10, 2001 - ECB cuts key rates by a quarter percentage point to 4.5 percent, the first in a series of 7 cuts that takes rates down to a record low of 2 percent by June 2003. Euro closes at $0.8812.   \par \par \uc2 * Jan 3, 2001 - U.S. Federal Reserve cuts the federal funds rate 50 basis points to 6.0 percent. The move marks start of aggressive interest rate cuts which take rates as low as 1.0 percent by June 2003. Euro closes at $0.9281.   \par \par \uc2 * Nov 10, Nov 6, Nov 3, 2000 - ECB in conjunction with national euro zone central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros, leaving the euro trading around $0.86.   \par \par \uc2 * Oct 26, 2000 - Euro hits record low of $0.8225, 30 percent below its launch value.   \par \par \uc2 * Sept 22, 2000 - Central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting together for first time since 1995, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive euro higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits low below $0.85 two days earlier. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bout is first by new ECB.   \par \par \uc2 * Dec 2, 1999 - Euro falls to one-for-one with the dollar for the first time. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg says euro has strong potential to appreciate.   \par \par \uc2 * Apr 8, 1999 - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes its first monetary policy move, lowering key refinancing rate 50 basis points to 2.50 percent. Euro closes at $1.0798.   \par \par \uc2 * Jan 4, 1999 - Euro begins trading at $1.1747, hitting a high of $1.1906 on the same day.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EURO (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070920e39k000ov\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
