\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sags, but firms against Canadian dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Plumberg  \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2004\par 21:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 7 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped broadly, but rose against the Canadian dollar on Tuesday after Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled its strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually affect domestic demand and prevent interest rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Canada opted to keep its overnight rate unchanged at 2.50 percent, citing the dampening effects of the stronger Canadian dollar. Higher interest rates usually attract foreign investors to invest in a particular country's assets, increasing the allure of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reaction "was off-center of the recent U.S. dollar trend," said Lara Rhame, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had only a few weeks ago hit a 12-year low against the Canadian dollar, on a general downtrend triggered by U.S. deficit concerns. The Bank of Canada's rate decision Tuesday pushed the U.S. dollar to a one-month high against the Canadian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the news out of the Bank of Canada or the dollar/Canada price action really alters anything about the U.S. (dollar) trend," added Rhame.  \par \par \uc2 "What it does do is really put the magnifying glass on how other central banks are already substituting a tighter monetary policy for a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts will focus on statements following Australian and New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings on Wednesday and Thursday respectively, to see if the U.S. dollar's weakness is affecting these countries in the same way.  \par \par \uc2 During the London trading trading session, the dollar slid to new lows near $1.3470 per euro as investors, tiring of tough talk on exchange rates by European officials, dismissed new warnings from euro zone finance ministers about the dangers of the rising euro.  \par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, European finance ministers meeting in Brussels late on Monday charged that "excessive volatility and disorderly movements" in exchange rates were hurting growth and added that they "will monitor the situation closely."  \par \par \uc2 These comments, which were the group's most stark warning to date on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , took on added weight with the backing of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts said the remarks echoed what euro zone finance ministers have been saying individually for some time and were unlikely to trouble the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has shown little inclination to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the dollar's slide.  \par \par \uc2 "The finance ministers are not really going beyond statements that have already been put out, and until something new is said, we will see the dollar going lower," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York.  \par \par \uc2 OH CANADA  \par \par \uc2 By mid-afternoon in New York, the euro had pared its gains but still was up around 0.2 percent against the dollar at $1.3427.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was up 0.35 percent at $1.9454, having earlier pushed above $1.95 for a new 12-year high. The dollar lost 0.1 percent against the Swiss franc to 1.1404 francs.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 0.3 percent to 102.87 yen.  \par \par \uc2 By late afternoon, the dollar was up 0.5 percent against the Canadian unit at C$1.2066, having earlier risen as high as C$1.2112.  \par \par \uc2 In a major reversal from earlier communiques, the Bank of Canada Tuesday dropped references to the need to reduce monetary stimulus further, suggesting it might slow down the pace of tightening.  \par \par \uc2 "If the Canadian economy can adjust at 80 U.S. cents, then the Bank of Canada can start raising rates," said Jeremy Friesen, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBC Capital Markets in Toronto.  \par \par \uc2 "But if not, it will have to do something that will result in Canadian dollar weakness - either a pause in the tightening cycle or, in the extreme, reversing course," he said. (Additional reporting by Andrea Ricci).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041207e0c700f1r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
