\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan says Europe, U.S. share concern over dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2004\par 10:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 2 (Reuters) - Japan insisted on Thursday that its concern about the dollar's steep decline was shared by European and U.S. authorities, keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market guessing about possible concerted action.  \par \par \uc2 A day after Tokyo's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy official suggested that Japan and Europe could jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the dollar's slide, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said all three economic powers shared concern about its fall.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki, speaking to reporters soon after the dollar fell below 102 yen for the first time in nearly five years, declined to comment on the possibility of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said movements in the market have been volatile.  \par \par \uc2 "We are in close contact with the euro authorities and the dollar authorities," Tanigaki told reporters, adding that the European authorities shared Japan's concern.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if Washington also shared it, he replied: "I think so."  \par \par \uc2 The comments come in response to the dollar's 10 percent slide in the last three months to record lows versus the euro and an 11 percent fall since mid-May to its weakest against the yen in nearly five years.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, Vice Finance Minister Koichi Hosokawa reiterated the government's standard line that it would "act boldly" against rapid moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 Hosokawa, however, has been sending a stronger message in recent weeks, saying the dollar's decline has deviated from economic fundamentals - a condition cited by the Japanese authorities for them to step in to curb the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 "The recent rise in the yen is rapid and it doesn't necessarily reflect fundamentals, so we are watching with very close attention," Hosokawa told a news conference.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WARNING  \par \par \uc2 Their comments come on the heels of remarks by Tokyo's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy bureaucrat, Hiroshi Watanabe, on Wednesday suggesting the possibility of joint European-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said conditions were "in place for Japan and Europe to be able to take harmonised action".  \par \par \uc2 The remark was met with suspicion in the market, which doubts whether the Group of Seven (G7) industrial nations have any consensus on how to deal with the dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "At current exchange rate levels, I think chances for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are slim," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "Japanese authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar dropped below 100 yen, but even then it won't be like last year's large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar fell as low as 101.83 yen  on Thursday, dipping below 102\par yen for the first time since March 2000. It also plumbed record lows\par against the euro of around $1.3385 to the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2         and\par 12-year lows beyond $1.94 to the pound       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But one of Watanabe's predecessors, Makoto Utsumi, told Reuters that a European-Japanese joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was possible and it was even conceivable that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could join in.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think we can assume that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a free hand to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market, while it is certain that the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is getting more nervous," said Utsumi, now president of Japan Credit Rating Agency.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Depending on the situation, there could be a scenario in which two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  zones jointly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first and then invite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to join in," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Washington could not leave everything to the market if it became evident that the dollar's weakness was triggering capital outflows from the U.S. markets, Utsumi said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he said that given a stronger economic performance and higher interest rates in the United States, the U.S. markets should continue to attract funds from around the world and the dollar would not fall much further.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others expressed doubts whether adjustments through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would correct the U.S. current account deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given productivity levels and international flows, there is something wrong about the theory that the deficit in the current account cannot be fixed without an adjustment in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates," BOJ Deputy Governor Kazumasa Iwata told a seminar. (Additional reporting by Ritsuko Ando, Shinichi Kishima and Tamawa Kadoya).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041202e0c20060r\par }\page 