\par\par \b \uc2 CHRONOLOGY-Milestones for euro since launch.  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,260 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2004\par 15:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 10 (Reuters) - The euro rose to a record high above $1.3000 on Wednesday as investors worried about structural problems in the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 Following is a chronology showing the fall and rise of Europe's nearly six-year-old single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 * January 1999 - Euro begins trading at $1.1747 on January 4 and rises on the same day to around $1.19, a level that remains its all-time high until May 2003. It then begins a decline fuelled both by dollar strength and by a perceived reluctance by the new European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) to respond to political and market pressure for lower interest rates to help economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 * March 1999 - German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine resigns. A few days later, the European Commission resigns en masse because of a financial mismanagement scandal. The euro slides three cents during the month to end at around $1.08.  \par \par \uc2 * April 1999 - Euro falls below $1.08 as the ECB makes the first monetary policy move of its career, lowering interest rates by half a percentage point to 2.50 percent.  \par \par \uc2 * December 1999 - Euro falls to one-for-one with the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 * September 2000 - Euro falls below $0.85. Central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing its weakness may destabilise the world economy.  \par \par \uc2 * October 2000 - Effect of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proves short-lived as euro slides to a record low of $0.8225, 30 percent below its launch value.  \par \par \uc2 * November 2000 - Euro rises to $0.86 as national central banks in the euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros alongside the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 * January 2001 - Euro rises above $0.95 as concerns mount over the state of the U.S. economy and the U.S. Federal Reserve cuts interest rates by half a point to 6 percent, the start of an aggressive rate-cutting campaign. The euro trades in a range between $0.84 and $0.95 for the next seven months.  \par \par \uc2 * September 2001 - Euro rises two percent to $0.92 on September 11 as two hijacked planes hit the twin towers of New York's World Trade Center. The euro eases towards the end of that year, ending 2001 around $0.88.  \par \par \uc2 * January 2002 - The euro leaps above $0.90 on the first trading day of the year, on enthusiasm inspired by the smooth introduction of euro notes and coins in the 12 countries of the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 * June 2002 - Euro extends gains above $0.96 as the United States shows a record current account deficit in the first quarter and U.S. telecommunications firm WorldCom and office equipment maker Xerox follow energy giant Enron in revealing accounting irregularities.  \par \par \uc2 * July 2002 - Euro regains parity with the dollar as concern over the outlook for Wall Street encourages investors to move out of U.S. assets. Euro zone finance ministers issue a formal statement saying they welcome the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 * December 2002 - Euro ends the month near $1.05, up almost 18 percent on the year on mounting concerns that geopolitical uncertainty will make it hard for the United States to attract enough foreign investment to fund its rising trade gap.  \par \par \uc2 * March 2003 - Euro rises above $1.10, spooked by the imminent U.S.-led war against Iraq but slips back to $1.06 in the first week of April on hopes for a speedy U.S. victory.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * April/early May 2003 - Euro gains 10 cents against the dollar in the\par space of five weeks, rising above its launch levels against the yen\par and British pound          . Dollar struggles across the board as\par worries over the U.S. current account and budget deficits resurface\par after the Iraq war.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * May 8, 2003 - ECB President Wim Duisenberg says there is nothing excessive about the euro's current level. Euro hits record high against sterling, above 71.72 pence.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * May 11, 2003 - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow says a cheap dollar would help U.S. exports, helping lift the euro above $1.16 for first time in over four years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * May 12, 2003 - Euro hits record high against yen, above 135 yen. Euro zone finance ministers and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issue a joint statement saying it is in the bloc's interest to have a strong and stable euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * May 23, 2003 - Euro rises above $1.1747 January 1999 launch level.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * May 27, 2003 - Euro hits record high above $1.1930. By the end of the month it has also hit a new record above 140 yen and 72.55 British pence.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * June 16, 2003 - Euro retests record high, its recent gains seen due to defensive buying of euro-denominated bonds, particularly by Asian investors, who worry about the global economy and the aftermath of the Iraq war.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * September 2003 - Euro hits 4-1/2 month low of $1.0760 as renewed optimism about a global economic recovery encourages buyers of U.S. equities and sellers of euro zone bonds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Sept 22, 2003 - Euro goes up 3/4 cent to $1.1445 at the Asian open after a weekend G7 meeting in Dubai calls for greater global exchange rate flexibility, which investors interpret as a green light to sell the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Nov 28, 2003 - Euro rises above $1.20 for first time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Dec 17, 2003 - Euro rises to a record of 104.38 on the ECB's euro index , but ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing says the euro rate corresponds to its long-term average.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Dec 31, 2003 - Euro climbs above $1.26 for first time, marking gains of five percent in past five weeks, more than 20 percent over the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Jan 12, 2004 - Euro hits record high of $1.2898. ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet says Europe is concerned about "brutal" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements and the euro falls a cent to $1.2799.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Feb 7, 2004 - Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers warn markets "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth". Euro opens on Feb. 9 at $1.2609, one cent below its close on Feb 6.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Feb 11, 2004 - U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan says in Congressional testimony the Fed can be "patient" in shifting from its accommodative policy stance. Euro jumps nearly two cents from the day's lows to a high of $1.2836.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Feb 18, 2004 - Euro breaks above $1.29 for first time, encouraged by apparent lack of resistance by ECB policymakers. It hits record high $1.2927.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Apr 26, 2004 - Euro hits a four-month low of $1.1759 as global investors bet economic recovery and interest rate hikes in the United States will give the dollar new strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Oct 15, 2004 - Euro breaks out of range trade which dominated through the summer months as investors start worrying the U.S. economic recovery will not be strong enough to attract the cash needed to plug the U.S. current account deficit. The dollar's problems are exacerbated by record high oil prices and speculation the U.S. is happy to see a weaker greenback.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Nov 3, 2004 - Euro gains roughly one U.S. cent after George W. Bush is re-elected for his second term in the White House. With election uncertainty now over, investors return to selling the dollar on concerns over structural problems in the U.S. economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Nov 10, 2004 - Euro rises to record high above $1.3000 after better-than-expected U.S. trade data fail to dispel worries over the current account deficit.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041110e0ba00b1g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
