\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar struggles above record lows, eyes payrolls.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2004\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 5 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled just above this year's record low against the euro on Friday amid worries about the health of the U.S. economy but caution ahead of U.S. jobs data later in the day limited its moves.  \par \par \uc2 With the U.S. presidential vote out of the way, the market has returned its focus to U.S. structural problems, particularly the U.S. current account deficit, powering a broad dollar selloff this week.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. payrolls data will add more key information about the health of the U.S. economy but analysts said only a very good number could help the dollar overcome its bearish trend.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think we will attack the record (euro/dollar) highs before the payrolls data. Payrolls are important," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "But even if payrolls are in line with expectations, a test of record levels is possible. We are in a hugely dollar sceptical environment and it would take a lot to get a counter-trend."  \par \par \uc2 At 0900 GMT the dollar traded at $1.2863 per euro, steady on the day, after rising to $1.2893 earlier in the session. This compared with Thursday's 8-1/2 month low of $1.2897 per euro and February's record low of $1.2927.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also held close to Thursday's near-seven month month low of 105.67 yen and an eight-year low of 1.1835 Swiss francs, also set on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 A slide in oil prices only marginally supported the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. light crude was at $48.50 a barrel, well down from the all-time high of $55.67 hit at the end of last month.  \par \par \uc2 LASTING PROBLEMS  \par \par \uc2 Investors' worries about the U.S. current account deficit have been aggravated by concerns over the U.S. economy, which needs to attract a lot of foreign capital to plug the external shortfall.  \par \par \uc2 "With all the focus on the current account deficit, sentiment is just overwhelmingly against the dollar right now," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of UFJ Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.  \par \par \uc2 A healthy jobs market is seen a crucial to maintaining the pace of growth in the U.S. economy, which is driven largely by domestic consumption.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed U.S. non-farm payrolls rose by 169,000 in October, compared with a rise of 96,000 in September.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the dollar's slide on Thursday was also helped by signs European policy-makers were little concerned about the rise in the euro, in stark contrast to February's barrage of comments criticising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet avoided making any comments about the current level of the euro during a news conference that followed Thursday's ECB meeting.  \par \par \uc2 PRESSURE IN ASIA  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also fell slightly on comments from a senior Japanese politician, who said that although the yen's recent rise had been rapid there was no need yet for Japan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to correct volatile movements.  \par \par \uc2 Kaoru Yosano, policy chief of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, also told Reuters he did not expect Washington to aggressively pursue a weaker dollar as that would prompt capital outflows from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Europe.  \par \par \uc2 Takeda of Tokyo-Mitsubishi said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a real threat at present as traders believe the Japanese authorities are aware of the difficulty in defending the yen amid the dollar's broad-based weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed has raised rates three times since June, bringing its overnight funds rate to 1.75 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation was also rising about a revaluation of the Chinese yuan after top officials from China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator were quoted saying they hoped to have a more flexible exchange rate and had made fundamental preparations.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041105e0b500aqb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
