\par\par \b \uc2 Global policymakers seem to accept weaker dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2004\par 20:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 25 (Reuters) - Global policy-makers may be starting to accept a weaker dollar as perhaps the only policy option left to correct the record U.S. external deficits and keep the world's economic recovery on track, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 The response from policy makers in the U.S., euro zone and Japan to the dollar's slide in recent weeks has broadly been one of acquiescence, which is actually helping fuel the selloff. Market participants cite anecdotal evidence that officials are happy to see an orderly decline in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve officials are becoming more vocal in their warnings that the U.S. current account gap, which is running at around $600 billion a year, is unsustainable. As the Fed doesn't normally comment on the dollar, which is considered the Treasury's territory, some of these comments have had a particularly big impact.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary John Snow advocates a "strong dollar" policy but only nominally, most observers say.  \par \par \uc2 Dallas Fed President Robert McTeer on Oct 7 said: "Over time, there is only one direction for the dollar to go - lower." And on Thursday last week San Francisco Fed President Janet Yellen said the dollar's value remains "relatively high" despite the ballooning trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The comments have fueled rumors of a coordinated effort by G7 central banks to bring the dollar lower," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Japanese authorities appear more sanguine about the yen's strength than they were earlier this year, and they have not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its rise.  \par \par \uc2 And euro-zone officials, who were also quick to express displeasure with the euro's potentially export-crimping surge to record levels in February, have also been quiet. Indeed, because a strong exchange rate dampens the inflationary threat in high oil prices, many have said the euro's current strength is not a problem.  \par \par \uc2 "Official commentary from outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests few near-term risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , verbal or otherwise," said Citibank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts in a note to clients on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 SOMETHING IN THE AIR, BUT NO DEAL  \par \par \uc2 In an environment of slowing economic growth, falling stock markets, record oil prices, and rising interest rates in many countries, policy-makers are in a bind.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is highly unlikely to cut interest rates to lower the euro because annual inflation is running above its 2 percent target.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, still wary of deflation, cannot lower interest rates which are already near zero to weaken the yen.  \par \par \uc2 More importantly, it may be U.S. policy-makers who are now in a fix, as they try to keep the engine of global economic growth going, while addressing the country's record trade and budget deficits.  \par \par \uc2 But they have little room for maneuver. There are no more tax cuts in the offing if President Bush is reelected, and monetary stimulus is being withdrawn as the Fed raises interest rates from 4-H'er lows.  \par \par \uc2 The insatiable appetite of American consumers for foreign goods means the trade deficit shows no sign of narrowing. The more it deteriorates, the greater the risk of a potentially brutal decline in equity and bond markets, and rise in interest rates, which would be a blow to the U.S. and world economy.  \par \par \uc2 As a result a market-led orderly depreciation in the dollar is perhaps the only option.  \par \par \uc2 Since the comments by regional Fed president McTeer, the dollar has slumped to multi-city - and in some cases, multiyear - lows against its major rivals. On Monday, the euro came within less than a cent of its all-time high of $1.2927 EUR=).  \par \par \uc2 An officially-sanctioned Group of Seven call for a weaker dollar, however, is some way off, and would only be considered if the U.S. fell into recession, said ABN Amro's Anderson.  \par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the U.S. has been pressing China to allow more flexibility in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, which is seen likely to appreciate if allowed to float freely and take some pressure of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan is currently pegged at 8.28 per dollar. Many of China's neighbors, as well as U.S. manufacturers, complain this gives Beijing an unfair competitive advantage on the global export markets.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have long said they'll adopt a more flexible exchange rate policy in time, but only when the country's financial system and economy is ready for it. In other words, they'll do so at the timing of their choosing.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041025e0ap00atv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
