\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Japan's recovery seen sustained, growth to firm.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Glenn Somerville  \par \uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2004\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 12 (Reuters) - Japan's first quarter economic growth probably didn't match the sizzling pace of the preceding quarter, a Reuters poll showed on Wednesday, but a senior U.S. official joined economists in declaring the recovery on track.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of 25 economists produced a forecast for an annualised growth rate of 3.6 percent in January-March.  \par \par \uc2 That would follow a 6.4 percent rate of expansion in the October-December quarter, the strongest quarter in 13 years.  \par \par \uc2 "The economy has turned the corner and what we're seeing now is some sustainability," U.S. Treasury Under-Secretary John Taylor told reporters in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "It's turned corners before, but this is turning the corner and moving down the road because there's a number of quarters of positive growth. It's not simply exports."  \par \par \uc2 While exports remain the main driver of growth, economists said a recovery in domestic demand appeared to be taking root.  \par \par \uc2 Strong corporate earnings are boosting capital spending and underpinning the labour market and household spending.  \par \par \uc2 "In our research report in March, we anticipated a corrective slowdown after the breakneck pace in October-December, but the adjustment has not turned out to be so significant and we're likely to see continued strength," said Tetsuro Sawano, senior fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Taylor said Japan was making progress not only in restoring economic growth, but in whittling down a mountain of bad loans accumulated over more than a decade of stop-go growth.  \par \par \uc2 Deflation was also being quelled, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Through a policy known as "quantitative easing", Japan has been flooding money markets with liquidity to keep interest rates near zero, trying to beat deflation. Taylor said it was impossible to say when the battle would be won.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no way to determine it precisely, it depends upon events," Taylor said, noting the Bank of Japan has clearly stated that it would maintain the policy until the year-on-year rise in the consumer price index stabilises above zero.  \par \par \uc2 EXIT STRATEGY  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ recently forecast that deflation would ease in the coming year as the recovery strengthened, bolstering expectations that policy may be reversed in the next two or three years.  \par \par \uc2 But it has also sought to dampen expectations of an early change, fearing a spike in interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ Policy Board member Shin Nakahara said on Wednesday the BOJ should make preparations to ensure a smooth exit from its ultra-loose policy, suggesting setting an ideal inflation rate.  \par \par \uc2 "One way to stabilise market expectations when we change our quantitative easing policy regime would be to indicate beforehand what sort of inflation rate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  finds appropriate," he told business leaders in the northern city of Akita.  \par \par \uc2 "I think that 1-2 percent would be an appropriate goal in the end," he said, but he added that such a move should not limit the BOJ's independence or flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 In meetings on Wednesday, Taylor heard business leaders report anecdotal signs of greater consumer willingness to spend and invest, a necessary condition for broadening the recovery.  \par \par \uc2 A government survey on Wednesday showed that more than 60 percent of Japanese consumers expected prices a year from now to rise between zero and two percent.  \par \par \uc2 Taylor declined a direct answer when asked whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  was pleased that the BOJ had cut back on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the yen from appreciating too rapidly.  \par \par \uc2 "We're pleased that the economy is picking up and that the monetary policy, a policy that has been dealing with purchasing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and domestic securities, is responding as the economy is picking up," Taylor said.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Ministry figures released on Wednesday showed Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign came to a halt in mid-March, effectively a change in market strategy.  \par \par \uc2 Japan spent a record $131 billion in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter the yen's strength in the first three months of the year but did not step into the market after March 16.  \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters Television, Taylor said he was not worried that a slower pace of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have an adverse impact on Japanese buying of U.S. securities.  \par \par \uc2 "We have never been concerned about the impact on U.S. securities," Taylor said.  \par \par \uc2 "The Treasury markets are broad and strong...and the particular investment strategies of central banks that accumulate reserves have not been a concern to us at all."  \par \par \uc2 Japan is one of the largest purchasers of U.S. securities, sending dollars to the United States to buy U.S. Treasuries and effectively helping finance huge U.S. budget deficits. (Additional reporting by Akiko Ishiwata, Tamawa Kadoya, Ritsuko Ando and Dan Sloan).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040512e05c001h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
