\par\par \b \uc2 Japan less stressed over G7 as economy, dollar rise.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ritsuko Ando  \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2004\par 09:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 16 (Reuters) - With Japan's economy growing at its fastest pace in 13 years and its controversial \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently winding down, Japanese officials may feel less defensive than usual at next week's Group of Seven meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "The previous few meetings were rather exceptional as the yen had been surging, the government was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and speculative forces were driving the market," said Naoki Murakami, senior economist at Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no pressure on Japan this time around."  \par \par \uc2 Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the leading economic powers meet in Washington at the end of next week.  \par \par \uc2 Geopolitical risks and the implications for the world economy will be the main focus this time but Japan will still need to be careful as the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could resurface at any time in the run-up to U.S. elections in November.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has been a key item at recent G7 meetings as the dollar tumbled against the euro and the yen and Japan sought to protect an export-led economic recovery. It has spent about 35 trillion yen ($327.7 billion) over the past 15 months to hold the yen down.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar has regained some strength in the past few weeks and financial markets believe Japan has scaled back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-March, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  less reason to complain.  \par \par \uc2 Supporting that view, the U.S. Treasury said in a report on Thursday that no major trading partner had manipulated its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain a trade advantage in the second half of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR WORDING  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui, who will attend the meeting with Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, has said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not likely to be a key issue.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said on Friday the G7 could discuss financial markets but only as part of talks about the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 All the same, markets will be watching for any change in the wording of the final statement from the last one issued in Florida in February, which called for flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy but also warned against excessive market volatility.  \par \par \uc2 Most expect no major change except for a possible mention of growing geopolitical risks.  \par \par \uc2 "They may agree to greater cooperation to control geopolitical risks, but the overall message doesn't need to change," said Shuji Shirota, an economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein.  \par \par \uc2 Some said Japan may try to excise the reference to "flexible" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, perceived to be a criticism of Japan as well as China, but the United States is not expected to allow that.  \par \par \uc2 Others said the statement's entire reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  ought to be simplified.  \par \par \uc2 "The part about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew long and convoluted because it took into account everyone's wishes. It could be trimmed," said Yoshimasa Maruyama, economist at Mizuho Research Institute. "But they've probably learned to be extra cautious."  \par \par \uc2 A G7 statement in Dubai last year triggered a surge in the yen as investors guessed - wrongly - that its call for flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would make it hard for Japan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Florida meeting in February soothed market nerves by adding a clause stating "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth".  \par \par \uc2 DEBT AND DEFLATION  \par \par \uc2 Japan's economy grew by an annualised 6.4 percent in October-December, outperforming the United States and Europe.  \par \par \uc2 Recent data have suggested the recovery was spreading to long-suffering small enterprises and non-manufacturers.  \par \par \uc2 Fukui and Tanigaki will not be able to rest on their laurels, however, as long as Japan languishes in debt and deflation.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's public debt is the highest among the G7 countries at an estimated 140 percent of gross domestic product, the result of years of public spending in a vain effort to reflate the economy.  \par \par \uc2 And deflation in Japan is entering its sixth year.  \par \par \uc2 For that reason, however, the rising oil and commodity prices worrying some countries are not such a problem for Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "Rising oil and commodities prices might be discussed at G7, but such trends have a mixed impact in Japan so it probably has little to say," said Mamoru Yamazaki, chief economist at Barclays Capital. "As far as this G7 goes, Japan is neither a source nor a lone casualty of any economic problem."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040416e04g00102\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
