\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar up on ECB interest rate cut speculation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 22:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes dateline; previous NEW YORK)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The dollar gained half a percent against the euro on Thursday, drawing support from heightened expectations that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut interest rates next week.  \par \par \uc2 In afternoon trading, the euro showed little reaction to comments from German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder, who reiterated that he would make euro strength against the dollar a main theme in talks with U.S. President George W. Bush on Friday. Earlier, the German leader said he was very concerned about recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations.  \par \par \uc2 German and French leaders' urging the ECB to cut interest rates to protect the fragile export sector has boosted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A rate cut would remove some of the euro's appeal, making yields on U.S. assets more attractive.  \par \par \uc2 "The level of uncertainty regarding potential ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ECB rate cuts has increased, and that has contributed to the euro's slide," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst at Ruesch International in Washington, D.C.  \par \par \uc2 "Also, the increased jawboning by top-level European officials has certainly spooked the market and has prompted the euro to retreat," he added.  \par \par \uc2 In late New York trade, the euro was down 0.5 percent against the dollar at $1.2429 after falling to a four-week low of $1.2385 after the release of U.S. durable goods data for January.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar traded up 0.56 percent on the yen at 109.63 yen  after\par hitting 109.83, its highest since early December. The dollar rose 0.64\par percent against the Swiss franc to 1.2674 francs       , while sterling\par fell 0.45 percent to $1.8612       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar pushed higher after January U.S. durable goods data suggested strength in the economy despite a disappointing headline figure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It has been a pretty choppy trading session, but the dollar has done a good job of not only sustaining yesterday's impressive rally, but even adding to it," said Beuzelin.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, all eyes will be on U.S. fourth-quarter gross domestic product, consumer sentiment and Midwest business data.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DURABLE GOODS DATA, EUROPEAN COMMENTS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar strengthened on news that U.S. non-defense capital goods orders excluding aircraft rose 3.6 percent last month after a 3.8 percent gain in December.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impact of an overall 1.8 percent decline in durable goods orders was lessened by a sharp upward revision for December to a 1.6 percent gain, giving investors another reason to buy the dollar. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was little affected by a gain in new weekly U.S. jobless claims.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market wanted to see a dollar positive here, and they were held back for a couple of seconds by the headline of durable goods. But once they looked beyond it, they saw what they wanted," said Steven Englander, chief North American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is still looking to buy dollars today. You are still getting some position-squaring," Englander said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also grew wary of buying euros after Schroeder on Wednesday urged the ECB to cut rates to help exporters and following a report that the ECB and the Bank of Japan were ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the euro topped $1.35.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Schroeder is on a two-day visit to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many in the market were skeptical any ECB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was imminent, but traders said a perception was growing that the dollar might have turned the corner after a two-year bear run.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin said on Thursday he shared Schroeder's view on European interest rates and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates were not in line with economic reality.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the ECB faces pressure to cut its official interest rate from 2.0 percent, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's positive outlook on the U.S. economy, expressed on Wednesday, stoked speculation that a U.S. rate hike from just 1.0 percent may come sooner, rather than later.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy in New York).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040226e02q00429\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
