\par\par \b \uc2 CHRONOLOGY-Milestones for euro since launch.  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,162 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2004\par 17:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 18 (Reuters) - The euro rose above $1.29  for the first time since its 1999 launch on Wednesday, encouraged by a lack of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resistance and the view that U.S. interest rates would stay low for a while.   \par \par \uc2  Following is a chronology showing the fall and rise of Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  * January 1999 - Euro begins trading at $1.1747 on January 4 and rises on the same day to around $1.19, a level that remains its all-time high until May 2003. It then begins a decline fuelled both by dollar strength and by a perceived reluctance by the new European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) to respond to political and market pressure for lower interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  * March 1999 - German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine resigns, citing policy differences. A few days later, the European Commission resigns en masse because of a financial mismanagement scandal. The euro slides three cents during the month to end at around $1.08.   \par \par \uc2  * April 1999 - Euro falls below $1.08 as the ECB makes the first monetary policy move of its career, lowering interest rates by half a percentage point to 2.50 percent.   \par \par \uc2  * December 1999 - Euro falls to one-for-one with the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  * September 2000 - Euro falls below $0.85. Central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing its weakness may destabilise the world economy.   \par \par \uc2  * October 2000 - Effect of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proves short-lived as euro slides to a record low of $0.8225, 30 percent below its launch value.   \par \par \uc2  * November 2000 - Euro rises to $0.86 as national central banks in the euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros alongside the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  * January 2001 - Euro rises above $0.95 as concerns mount over the state of the U.S. economy and the U.S. Federal Reserve cuts interest rates by half a point to 6.0 percent, the start of an aggressive rate-cutting campaign. The euro trades in a range between $0.84 and $0.95 for the next seven months.   \par \par \uc2  * September 2001 - Euro rises two percent to $0.92 on September 11 as two hijacked planes hit the twin towers of New York's World Trade Center. The euro eases towards the end of that year, ending 2001 around $0.88.   \par \par \uc2  * January, 2002 - The euro leaps above $0.90 on the first trading day of the year, on enthusiasm inspired by the smooth introduction of euro notes and coins in the 12 countries of the euro zone.   \par \par \uc2  * June 2002 - Euro extends gains above $0.96 as the United States shows a record current account deficit in the first quarter and U.S. telecommunications firm WorldCom and office equipment maker Xerox follow energy giant Enron in revealing accounting irregularities.   \par \par \uc2  * July 2002 - Euro regains parity with the dollar as concern over the outlook for Wall Street encourages investors to move out of U.S. assets. Euro zone finance ministers issue a formal statement saying they welcome the euro's rise.   \par \par \uc2  * December 2002 - Euro ends the month near $1.05, up almost 18 percent on the year on mounting concerns that geopolitical uncertainty will make it hard for the United States to attract enough foreign investment to fund its rising trade gap.   \par \par \uc2  * March 2003 - Euro rises above $1.10, spooked by the imminent U.S.-led war against Iraq but slips back to $1.06 in the first week of April as a speedy U.S. victory looks likely.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * April/early May 2003 - Euro gains 10 cents against the dollar in the\par space of five weeks, rising above its launch levels against the yen\par and British pound          . Dollar struggles across the board as\par worries over the U.S. current account and budget deficits resurface\par after the Iraq war.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * May 8 2003 - ECB President Wim Duisenberg says there is nothing\par excessive about the euro's current level. Euro hits record high against\par sterling, above 71.72 pence.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * May 11 2003 - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow notes a cheap dollar\par would help U.S. exports, helping lift the euro above $1.16 for first\par time in over four years.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * May 12 2003 - Euro hits record high against yen, above 135 yen. Euro\par zone finance ministers and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issue a joint\par statement saying it is in the bloc's interest to have a strong and\par stable euro.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * May 19 2003 - Euro comes within spitting distance of $1.1747 launch\par level after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow says after a weekend G7\par finance ministers meeting that recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  realignments were\par "fairly modest", re-enforcing speculation he was happy with a weak\par dollar.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * May 23 2003 - Euro rises above $1.1747 January 1999 launch level.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * May 27 2003 - Euro hits record high above $1.1930. By the end of the\par month it has also hit a new record above 140 yen and 72.55 British\par pence.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * June 16 2003 - Euro retests record high, its recent gains seen due to\par defensive buying of euro-denominated bonds, particularly by Asian\par investors, as worries over the global economy and the aftermath of the\par Iraq war continue.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * September 2003 - Euro hits 4-1/2 month low of $1.0760 as renewed\par optimism about a global economic recovery encourages buyers of U.S.\par equities and sellers of euro zone bonds.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * September 22 2003 - Euro goes up 3/4 cent to $1.1445 at the Asian\par open after a weekend G7 meeting in Dubai calls for greater global\par exchange rate flexibility, which investors interpret as a green light\par to sell the dollar.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * November 19 2003 - Euro hits record high of $1.1959 on concerns about\par geopolitical tensions and the U.S. current account deficit.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * November 28 2003 - Euro rises above $1.20 for first time.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * November 28-December 30 2003 - Euro hits record highs against dollar\par on an almost daily basis, provoking little reaction from European\par policymakers.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * December 17 - Euro hits a record high of 104.38 on the ECB's euro\par index , ECB chief economist Otmar Issing says the euro's exchange\par corresponded to its long-term average.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * December 31 2003 - Euro climbs above $1.26 for first time, marking\par gains of five percent in past five weeks, more than 20 percent over the\par year.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * January 12, 2004 - Euro hits record high of $1.2898. ECB President\par Jean-Claude Trichet says Europe is concerned about "brutal" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par movements, euro falls a cent to $1.2799.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * February 7, 2004 - Group of Seven finance ministers and central\par bankers warn markets "excess volatility and disorderly movements in\par exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth". Euro opens on\par February 9 at $1.2609, one cent below its close on Feb 6.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * February 11, 2004 - U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan says\par in Congressional testimony the Fed can be "patient" in shifting from\par its accommodative policy stance. Euro jumps nearly two cents from the\par day's lows to a high of $1.2836.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040218e02i003ej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
