\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Berlusconi says high euro is squeezing exports.  \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 19:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 ROME, Jan 28 (Reuters) - Italian Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi said on Wednesday that the strong euro was squeezing exports and that he hoped for dollar-euro parity in the future.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro is worth $1.20 and that provokes a reduction in exports," he said during a meeting with Italian fashion designers. "The big American market, which sells in dollars and buys in euros, has led to the squeezing of exports," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was trading at around $1.2633 at 1800 GMT on Wednesday and is up some 20 percent against the dollar since the start of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 "I hope the European economy knows how to react and that the difference between the euro and the dollar diminishes and we head towards a future in which there will be parity between the euro and the dollar," Berlusconi said.  \par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement said earlier on Wednesday the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  need not at present use a rate cut or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the euro, but urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address the dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Italian exporters, who built fortunes on the back of repeated devaluations of the old lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have bemoaned the recent euro strength and fear that it will put a further clamp on the sluggish economy.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040128e01s0036k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
