\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Clement-no need for ECB rate cut,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  now.  \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 13:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     BERLIN, Jan 28 (Reuters) - German Economy Minister Wolfgang\par Clement said on Wednesday the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  need not at\par present use a rate cut or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the euro, but\par urged the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to address the dollar's weakness.\par     Asked at a news conference in Berlin whether the German\par government was appealing to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to trim borrowing\par costs or to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the markets, Clement said:\par  \par     "I would not understand it in that way and in any case there\par is no need for it at the moment."\par     Clement said he was confident the ECB was closely monitoring\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the euro's exchange rate and that he had discussed the matter  \f28 \par \par \uc2 with ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet at the World Economic  \par \par \uc2 Forum in Davos, Switzerland.  \par \par \uc2 "I am sure, also after a short discussion with ECB President  \par \par \uc2 Trichet, that this development will be very closely watched by  \par \par \uc2 the ECB," Clement said.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "We have seen a certain calming of the situation after some\par very sporadic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements," Clement added.\par     "I am sure there is a pain barrier for German exporters,\par which I will not name here, but I expect the ECB will recognise\par it at the right time," he said.\par     Speaking on the sidelines of the news conference, Clement\par said: "The U.S. should also contribute to doing something\par against the dollar's decline."\par     However, he added that a global economic recovery was strong\par enough to withstand potential risks from the greenback's slide.\par     Clements comments came after a Reuters poll of economists on\par Wednesday suggested that if the euro continued its ascent\par against the dollar, an ECB rate cut could be on the cards.\par     However, most economists said they expect the ECB to wait\par until late this year or early 2005 before hiking rates.\par     Economists said the ECB could be impelled to cut rates if\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the euro resumed its rally against the dollar to reach  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 levels around $1.35. However, most thought this was unlikely to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 happen in the next six months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro zone policymakers have talked the euro down from a high  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of nearly $1.29 hit on January 12 and it was trading around  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 $1.26 on Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central bankers and finance ministers from the Group of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seven industrial nations are due to meet in Florida on February  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6-7. Financial markets are waiting to see whether ministers  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 agree to work together to curb the euro's rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040128e01s001yb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
