\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-ECB's Wellink happy with less volatile euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Amran Abocar  \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 00:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO, Jan 27 (Reuters) - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Nout Wellink said on Tuesday he was satisfied with reduced volatility in the euro and expects inflation to return to the bank's target this year.  \par \par \uc2 "My feeling is that in the recent period, the euro is more stable than before because it's moving within a certain range at this very moment, so I'm happy with the present developments," he said in an interview before a speech to a Toronto business group.  \par \par \uc2 Wellink, who is also head of the Dutch \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that while the stronger euro hampered exports, it helped dull inflation, which should lead to higher disposable incomes and consumption.  \par \par \uc2 He said he expected eurozone inflation to fall below 2 percent "in the coming period but most certainly within 2004."  \par \par \uc2 Wellink noted that while the stronger euro is putting pressure on exports, a faster-than-expected pick-up in global trade was serving as a balance to that.  \par \par \uc2 The euro , trading at $1.2644 to the dollar on Tuesday evening, has gained 20 percent against the greenback in the last year.  \par \par \uc2 There is speculation the ECB could cut interest rates to curb the euro's rise against the dollar or, alternatively, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 European ministers want the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help efforts to stem the euro's gains and want \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements to be discussed ahead of Group of Seven rich-nations meeting in Florida next month.  \par \par \uc2 Wellink declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said, in his opinion, the G7 did not need to issue a statement on exchange rates as it did at a meeting in Dubai last September.  \par \par \uc2 "My personal feeling is it's not really necessary to come out with a joint statement now," he said. "My feeling is we should be very careful in delivering formal statements as to what should happen with the exchange rate configuration in the world unless there is a very good reason for it."  \par \par \uc2 The central banker said he was encouraged by an upbeat read on the German Ifo business confidence index, which rose to 97.4 in January - the highest read since January 2001 - from an upwardly revised 96.9 in December.  \par \par \uc2 "It proved the European economy is coming back on track," he said. "It's more or less in line with our expectations so there's no need to immediately change the forecast. We expected an acceleration of growth and this is still the most plausible outcome."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040127e01r008r4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
