\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro retreats from highs after ECB shows concern.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 07:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The euro inched down against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday after European officials expressed concern about excessive movement in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Addressing the euro's overall climb against the dollar, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Jean-Claude Trichet told central bankers in Basel on Monday that "excess volatility and brutal moves were not welcome and not appropriate".   \par \par \uc2  Market participants, already growing doubtful about the amount of room left for the euro to rise, started altering their positions.   \par \par \uc2  "Our stance had already been to take profits on the euro's rise as the euro is at excessively high levels," said Yuuki Sakurai, general manager at Fukoku Mutual Life Insurance's finance and investment planning department.   \par \par \uc2  "While the dollar's downtrend is unlikely to stop anytime soon, I think many investors will also start adjusting their long-euro positions after Trichet's remarks from Basel," he said.   \par \par \uc2  At 0219 GMT, the euro stood around $1.2740 , down from $1.2751/56 in late U.S. trade. The greenback fell to record lows of $1.2899 in New York before Trichet's comments pulled it up.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 135.70 yen , down from around 136 yen in late New York trade.   \par \par \uc2  While Trichet's comments gave the dollar a much-needed boost against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some traders said that the rise was mainly due to repositioning, and questioned how long the respite would last.   \par \par \uc2  French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin said officials must act quickly to bring the euro-dollar into line, but traders also ponder whether the ECB would actually move to halt a further rise in the euro.   \par \par \uc2  Last week, ECB sources said that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be futile, and there was no sense that Washington was keen to use the meeting of the Group of Seven on February 6-7 to reverse recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trends.   \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's downward trend should continue, as real money flows are leaning on the selling side," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist as JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2  "Unless there's a major event, it shouldn't really change anything."   \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR/YEN STUCK   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading around 106.55 yen , down slightly from 106.65 yen in late U.S. trade.   \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen trading was confined to tight ranges on continuing concerns about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves had been rapid given U.S. economic fundamentals and that Japan would take steps against speculative movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2  In an effort to protect exporters' profits, Japanese authorities were thought to have poured up to four trillion yen ($37.50 billion) into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week.   \par \par \uc2  But given the dollar's recent overall weakness, traders were feeling pressure from both above and below, and expected the dollar to be stuck in narrow ranges versus the yen for some time.   \par \par \uc2  The market was awaiting comments from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, who is scheduled to make a speech in Berlin on globalisation at 1600 GMT.   \par \par \uc2  In particular, dealers were on the lookout for comments relating to U.S. interest rates in light of a much smaller-than-expected rise in December employment figures.   \par \par \uc2  Non-farm payrolls rose by just 1,000 last month compared with forecasts for an increase of around 145,000.   \par \par \uc2  "Whatever Greenspan says probably won't have a positive effect on the dollar. But all the same, it shouldn't be too negative," JP Morgan's Sasaki said. ($1=106.64 yen) ((Reporting by Mariko Hayashibara, Naomi Tajitsu and Shinichi Kishima; editing by Brent Kininmont; Reuters messaging; mariko.hayashibara.reuters.com@reuters.net; +81-3-3432-8478)).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d001h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
