\par\par \b \uc2 BC-TEXT-Treasury exchange rate policies report.  \b0\par\par\uc2 3,979 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2003\par 16:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     WASHINGTON, Oct 30 (Reuters) - Following is the full text\par of the U.S. Treasury Department's "Report to Congress on\par International Economic and Exchange Rate Policies, October\par 2003" released on Thursday:\par "This report reviews developments in international economic\par policy, including exchange rate policy, focusing on the first\par half of 2003.  The report is required under the Omnibus Trade\par and Competitiveness Act of 1988, which states that The\par Secretary of the Treasury shall analyze on an annual basis the\par exchange rate policies of foreign countries, in consultation\par with the International Monetary Fund, and consider whether\par countries manipulate the rate of exchange between their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the United States dollar for purposes of\par preventing effective balance of payments adjustments or gaining\par unfair competitive advantage in international trade.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After reviewing developments in the United States, the report\par examines exchange rate policies in major countries across five\par regions of the world: (1) the Americas, (2) Europe and Eurasia,\par (3) Africa, (4) the Middle East and South Asia, and (5) East\par Asia.  To summarize, the report finds that\par Countries around the world continue to follow a variety of\par exchange rate policies, ranging from a flexible exchange rate\par with little or no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unions and full\par dollarization.  For example, Canada follows a flexible exchange\par rate regime with no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Greece recently joined the\par European Monetary Union, and El Salvador maintained its\par dollarization regime.\par A notable trend observed over the past several years is the\par move by many countries to adopt flexible exchange rates,\par combined with clear price stability goals and a transparent\par system for adjusting monetary policy instruments. It is good\par news that nearly 100 countries have eschewed pegged exchange\par rates and have chosen to use a flexible exchange rate,\par dollarize, join a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  union, or create a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board.\par The report finds that no major trading partner of the United\par States meets the technical requirements for designation under\par the Omnibus Trade and Competitiveness Act of 1988 during the\par first half of 2003.  The report notes that while a number of\par countries continue to use pegged exchange rates and/or\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, a peg or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par does not in and of itself satisfy the statutory test.  Treasury\par has consulted with the IMF management and staff, as required by\par the statute, and they concur with our conclusions.  The\par Administration strongly believes that a system of flexible,\par market-based exchange rates is best for major trading partners\par of the United States.\par For this reason, Treasury is continuing to engage actively with\par countries and to encourage, in both bilateral and multilateral\par discussions, policies that promote a flexible market-based\par exchange rate combined with a clear price stability goal and a\par transparent system for adjusting policy instruments.  For\par example, one important accomplishment was the communique of the\par G7 Finance Ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governors in September of\par this year endorsing an Agenda for Growth and stating that more\par flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries\par or economic areas to promote smooth and widespread adjustments\par in the international financial system, based on market\par mechanisms.\par The United States International Accounts\par In assessing the trade deficits and surpluses of individual\par countries with the United States, it is important to examine\par first the overall U.S. trade and current account balance.\par Saving, Investment, and the Current Account\par Developments in the overall trade or current account balance\par are best understood in terms of developments in investment and\par saving.  The current account deficit is conceptually equal to\par the gap between investment and saving as a matter of\par international accounting.  When investment in the United States\par is higher than domestic saving, then foreigners make up the\par difference, and the United States has a current account\par deficit.  In contrast if savings exceed investments in a\par country, then that country has a current account surplus as its\par people invest abroad.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the second quarter of 2003, for example, the U.S. current\par account deficit was $570 billion (at a seasonally adjusted\par annual rate and adjusted to a national income accounting basis)\par or 5.3 percent of GDP.  This $570 billion deficit equaled the\par gap between $1,977 billion in investment and $1,407 billion in\par saving .  That is, U.S. domestic investment was $570 billion\par more than domestic saving with net foreign investment making up\par the difference.\par Viewed in these terms over the past year, the increase in the\par U.S. current account deficit was caused by an increase in\par investment, combined with a somewhat smaller decline in saving.\par Over the year ending in the second quarter of 2003 the current\par account deficit rose by $73 billion from the second quarter of\par 2002.  During that same period investment rose by $40 billion\par and saving fell by $33 billion.  The increase in investment\par reflected the acceleration in U.S. economic growth during 2003.\par  Overall, real GDP grew at a 3.3 percent annual rate in the\par second quarter, up from 1.4 percent in the first quarter.\par Business investment in equipment and software and residential\par investment posted large gains, and forward momentum in those\par sectors continued into the third quarter.\par The current account was $555 billion in deficit on a balance of\par payments basis in the first half of 2003.  How has the deficit\par been financed?  The largest single part of financing the\par current account deficit has recently been net private foreign\par purchases of U.S. securities, which reached $436 billion in the\par first half of 2003.  (Included in these were net foreign\par purchases of U.S Treasuries amounting to $151 billion.)  In\par addition, foreign official institutions increased their U.S.\par assets by $197 billion.  These items alone more than covered\par current account financing needs and the total of all other\par financing items were equal to a net $78 billion capital\par outflow.\par Due to the current account deficit the net investment position\par of the United States (with direct investment valued at the\par current stock market value of owners' equity) fell to a\par negative $2.6 trillion at the end of 2002 from a negative $2.3\par trillion at the end of 2001.  Despite this large negative\par position, net income payments on investment assets were not\par significant in 2002, amounting to roughly $1 billion.  These\par positive net income receipts are the result of large net\par inflows of income from direct investment offsetting net\par outflows of income on portfolio investment.\par The U.S. Dollar\par According to the Federal Reserve Board's broad nominal dollar\par index, the U.S. dollar registered a 5.1 percent depreciation\par during 2003H1.  The decline was 8.3 percent against the major\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (largely the industrialized countries) and\par 1.5 percent against other important trading partners (largely\par emerging market economies).  The broad index declined 9.2\par percent from February 27, 2002, when it reached its recent\par peak, through June 30, 2003.\par  Inflation remained subdued during this period. The consumer\par price index rose 2.1 percent over the 12 months ending in June\par as energy prices increased, while the core rate (excluding food\par and energy) increased 1.5 percent.  The Federal Reserve\par maintained the federal funds rate target at 1.25 percent\par through the first five months of the year, then lowered the\par rate to 1.0 percent in June. During 2003H1, U.S. interest rates\par declined toward historical lows, and interest rate\par differentials increasingly favored relatively higher yielding\par non-dollar placements.\par As discussed below, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of different economies\par showed varying degrees of flexibility relative to the dollar,\par as some monetary authorities sought to dampen or prevent\par movements of their exchange rates against the dollar while\par others did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at all.  The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  did not\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets during the first half of\par 2003.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Poland, for example, the zloty weakened in both nominal\par terms (1.9 percent versus the dollar and 10.5 percent versus\par the euro) and real terms during the period as the fiscal\par deficit grew and confidence in the zloty faltered.  The Czech\par Republic koruna depreciated in real terms due to deflation\par during the period and political uncertainty.  In Hungary the\par forint saw extreme volatility against the euro.  A strong\par appreciation in the beginning of the year prompted massive\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Hungary.  However,\par deteriorating macroeconomic conditions and market uncertainty\par concerning the course of fiscal reform led to capital flight\par and depreciation.  The Bulgarian lev appreciated against the\par dollar as its value was fixed relative to the euro as part of\par its successful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board arrangement.\par Africa\par Most key exchange rates in Africa depreciated against the U.S.\par dollar on a nominal basis during the first half of 2003.\par Notable exceptions were the South African rand, which\par appreciated 14.7 percent, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the CFA zone,\par which are pegged to the euro.  They appreciated against the\par dollar just as the euro did.  The strengthening of the rand\par probably reflected favorable interest rate differentials,\par improved investor sentiment following credit ratings upgrades,\par and the country's more sound economic fundamentals.\par GDP growth in Africa remained resilient despite the weak pace\par of recovery in industrialized countries.  For the continent as\par a whole, growth is expected to increase in 2003.  The\par continent's overall current account deficit is expected to fall\par slightly in 2003 from 1.3 percent of GDP in 2002, though\par current account deficits in many countries in sub-Saharan\par Africa remain relatively high.  Capital inflows are expected to\par increase, particularly from private sources.  Since 2001,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves have increased from $64.7 billion to\par a projected $86.7 billion in 2003, with the bulk of the reserve\par accumulation occurring in North African countries due to higher\par energy prices.\par In Egypt the Egyptian pound is notionally floating but strongly\par managed.   The pound did move substantially over the period,\par however, falling 23 percent against the dollar and 25 percent\par on a real trade-weighted basis.  The current account surplus\par grew during the first half of 2003, reaching 1.5 percent of GDP\par in the second quarter compared to 0.4 percent in the same\par quarter the year before, due in part to a slight contraction in\par imports but more significantly to an increase in both oil and\par non-oil exports.\par Middle East and South Asia\par The Middle East has seen strong growth this year, largely\par fueled by high global oil prices. Regional growth in 2003\par should end up greater than last year's 3.9 percent, with the\par growth largely centered in the oil-exporting countries.\par Many countries in this region maintain inflexible exchange rate\par regimes, including narrow bands and explicit or implicit pegs\par to the dollar.  The high price of oil has led to higher current\par account surpluses and official reserve accumulation among the\par oil exporters. In Saudi Arabia, for example, reserves increased\par 12 percent in the first four months of this year to reach $23.1\par billion, the highest level in a decade.  The current account\par surplus is expected to increase well above the 6.2 percent of\par GDP level in 2002.  Among the non-oil exporting countries, the\par current account deficit is expected to widen in 2003 from the\par 0.5 percent of GDP deficit recorded in 2002. In addition to\par higher oil prices, these countries were also hit by declines in\par tourism revenues due to the uncertainty surrounding the\par conflict in Iraq.\par India maintains a heavily managed exchange rate regime.  While\par the rupee appreciated 3.4 percent against the dollar in the\par first half of 2003, the Indian authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to limit\par further appreciation.  As a result, foreign reserves grew $11.6\par billion in the first half of the year, reaching $78 billion by\par end-June.  The current account returned to a small deficit\par (seasonally adjusted) after surpluses in 2002.  Capital\par inflows, including FDI, increased due to the improved economic\par outlook and expectations of an appreciation of the rupee.\par Turkey and Israel have flexible exchange rates. Both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par appreciated significantly since the start of the year, buoyed\par by strong investor demand for emerging market assets. The\par Turkish lira appreciated 18.3 percent between the end of\par December 2002 and the end of June 2003, following a 12.7\par percent depreciation in 2002.  The current account deficit fell\par to 1.6 percent of GDP in the first 6 months of 2003 from 3.0\par percent of GDP in the second half of 2002.  Gross \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves grew $2.1 billion over the period, reaching\par $28.8 billion by end-June.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Similarly the new Israeli shekel appreciated 9.8 percent from\par end-December 2002 to end-June 2003, following a 7.3 percent\par depreciation in 2002. The current account balance grew to a 1.8\par percent of GDP surplus in the period, compared to a 1.0 percent\par deficit in 2002. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves grew 2.0 percent\par over the period, reaching $24.6 billion by end-June.\par East Asia\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were generally stable against the dollar in\par the first half of 2003, although the Indonesian rupiah and Thai\par baht both appreciated near the end of the period.   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par stability was maintained against the backdrop of a high degree\par of regional economic uncertainty, stemming from SARS and oil\par prices.  Uncertainties regarding the oil price outlook were\par reduced after the start of the military campaign in Iraq.\par Efforts to contain SARS proved successful, and its economic\par effect, while significant for some countries, was less than\par anticipated.\par Japan\par Japan's current account surplus grew to $62 billion (3.0\par percent of GDP) in the first half of 2003, up from $54 billion\par (2.6 percent of GDP) in the second half of 2002.   While\par Japan's merchandise trade surplus declined by 9 percent during\par the period to $45.2 billion, Japan's services deficit narrowed\par substantially to $15.4 billion, 35 percent smaller than in the\par second half of 2002. Japan had a bilateral merchandise trade\par surplus with the United States of $32.2 billion during 2003H1,\par down from $36.8 billion in 2002H2, and from $33.1 billion in\par 2002H1.\par The persistent Japanese global current account surplus reflects\par the high rate of Japanese domestic saving relative to domestic\par investment.  The trend rate of Japanese growth fell after the\par first oil crisis, and again after the bursting of Japan's asset\par price bubble at the beginning of the 1990s.  Slower growth has\par meant lower investment, and the share of private investment in\par GDP has fallen from 26 percent in the 1960s, to 22 percent  in\par the 1980s, to 19 percent in the last 3 years.  Excess private\par saving has been partially absorbed by larger government\par deficits.\par During the first half of 2003, the yen fluctuated in a narrow\par range against the dollar, depreciating 0.9 percent to 119.9 at\par the end of June 2003 from 118.8 at the end of December 2002.\par But in September and October of this year the yen appreciated\par against the dollar to around the 108 level or about 7 percent\par since the beginning of September.   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par Japanese monetary authorities in the exchange markets totaled\par $59.0 billion in the first half of 2003 and continued in July\par and August with an additional $22.7 billion. Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves grew by 17 percent to $527 billion at the end\par of June, up from $451 billion at the end of December 2002.\par Taking the longer view over the past 3 years it has fluctuated\par between a low of 135 and a high of 108.  The Japanese have\par stated that their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is carried out when excess\par volatility or over-shooting is observed in the markets.  The\par Treasury is actively engaged with the Japanese on these issues\par both bilaterally and through the meetings of the G7 finance\par ministry and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials.\par China\par China's overall trade surplus narrowed in the first half of\par 2003 to $13.5 billion (2.2 percent of GDP), compared to $20.7\par billion a year earlier (3.8 percent of GDP). The reduced\par overall trade surplus was accounted for by the strong growth in\par China's merchandise imports compared to exports. This reflected\par China's growing deficit with trading partners other than the\par United States.  Other Asian economies that used to export\par directly to the United States increasingly ship components to\par China for assembly.  China's bilateral surplus on trade in\par goods with the United States grew to $53.9 billion in 2003H1,\par up from $43.1 billion a year earlier.\par China's current account surplus in 2003H1 was $11.1 billion\par compared to $13.6 billion in 2002H1. However, net FDI inflows\par were $26.9 billion, while non-FDI net capital inflows were\par $17.5 billion, compared to $22.7 billion and -$10.4 billion a\par  \par year earlier, respectively.    Thus, the increase in China\par official reserves was due more to capital inflows than to the\par much smaller current account surplus.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China has pegged its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since 1994 at 8.28 to the dollar.\par This policy is not appropriate for a major economy like China\par and should be changed.  The Administration has engaged in\par direct talks calling on the Chinese to move towards a flexible\par exchange regime.  The Administration has utilized bilateral and\par multilateral forums to urge China to move toward greater\par flexibility.  At the September 2003 G7 meeting in Dubai, the\par ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors endorsed flexibility in\par exchange rates for large economies.  Secretary Snow traveled to\par China for discussions with senior Chinese officials on\par financial issues including exchange rate policy. President\par Bush, in his bilateral meeting with President Hu, discussed\par China's exchange rate policy.\par China has maintained its fixed exchange rate despite\par significant changes  up and down  in the exchange rate of the\par dollar vis-`-vis other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  Recently there has been\par strong pressure on the yuan, as evidenced by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves rising to $346 billion in June 2003, up 43 percent\par from June 2002.  The Chinese government has indicated that it\par will move to a flexible exchange rate regime.  It is taking\par steps to get there and the U.S. is providing technical\par cooperation.  However, it also has stated that doing so\par immediately would harm the banking system and the overall\par Chinese economy.\par In his discussions, Secretary Snow stressed the desirability of\par China's moving to a flexible market-based exchange rate regime\par and reducing controls on capital flows.  The Chinese economy is\par now one of the largest in the world, and China is a major\par player in world trade.  The Chinese capital account is expected\par to become increasingly open, and policies of maintaining a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg in the face of an increasingly open capital\par account increases risk.  Greater exchange rate flexibility\par would also allow China greater scope to maintain a\par low-inflation, pro-growth monetary policy.  It would improve\par the allocation of resources and the quality of financial\par intermediation in the Chinese economy.   Moreover, such a\par change would be consistent with our view that the international\par trading system works best with free trade, the free flow of\par capital and with market-based exchange rates.\par Serious engagement with China on these issues will continue.  A\par new Technical Cooperation Program is being implemented under\par the leadership of the Secretary of the Treasury and the\par Governor of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of China.  This program can help\par create the market mechanisms needed to make the transition to a\par flexible exchange rate regime.  Moreover, the Chinese deputy\par finance minister and deputy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor have begun to\par have regular meetings with the G7 finance deputies, with the\par first meeting held in Dubai in September and the next one\par scheduled for Paris in November.\par Korea\par Korea entered its first recession in five years during the\par first half of 2003.  Real GDP contracted 2.9 percent in the\par second quarter of 2003, continuing the 1.6 percent drop in the\par first quarter.  The slowdown was due to contracting domestic\par demand combined with slowing external sales.  In response, both\par fiscal policy and monetary policy have been eased.  In July the\par overnight call rate was cut a quarter-point to 3.75 percent.\par Korea's current account surplus for the first half of 2003 was\par $1.2 billion, down from $3.9 billion for the same period last\par year.  The balance on capital and financial account\par transactions recorded an almost $3.0 billion increase as the\par result of portfolio and other investment inflows. Official\par foreign reserves increased by $10.2 billion to $131.7 billion.\par The U.S. bilateral trade deficit with Korea in the first half\par of 2003 was $5.4 billion.\par The won depreciated a net 0.8 percent in the first half of 2003\par but has been on a rising path for several months.  JP Morgan's\par real trade-weighted index of the won fell 2.3 percent during\par the period. While Korea has officially adopted a flexible\par exchange rate policy, in practice the won is quite closely\par linked to the Japanese yen.  Since 2000 the monthly nominal\par won/yen exchange rate has averaged 1031, and the rate has\par deviated no more than 6 percent from this average.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Taiwan\par After decreasing 2.4 percent in the first quarter of 2003,\par Taiwan's real GDP declined by a sharp 9.9 percent in the second\par quarter of 2003, reflecting the drag of SARS on the economy.\par Deflation remained entrenched, with consumer prices falling 2.2\par percent in 2002 and 0.9 percent in the first half of 2003.\par Taiwan recorded a current account surplus of about 10.6 percent\par of GDP or $14.7 billion during the first half of 2003. The\par current account surplus was due mainly to a large, $13.4\par billion, trade surplus with China and growing foreign\par investment earnings. The United States ran a $7.3 billion\par bilateral trade deficit with Taiwan in the first half of 2003.\par Net portfolio investment recorded an extraordinary deficit of\par 20 percent of GDP during the first half of 2003, as residents'\par portfolio investment abroad increased 160 percent compared with\par the same period in 2002.   These portfolio outflows were\par largely offset by other investment inflows, and the financial\par account recorded a more moderate deficit of 5 percent of GDP in\par the first half of 2003.   In the first half of 2003, the new\par Taiwan dollar appreciated slightly (0.3 percent) against the\par U.S. dollar and also depreciated 4.3 percent on a real trade\par weighted basis, as measured by the JP Morgan index.  Reserves\par increased by $15 billion during the first half of 2003.\par Taiwan's $177 billion in reserves are equivalent to almost 10\par times its short-term external debt and almost twice the\par country's total foreign liabilities.\par Taiwan is in the process of liberalizing its capital account\par regime; this liberalization has already resulted in a\par significant increase of flows in the financial account.  The\par increase in outward portfolio investment over the last 18\par months has eased appreciation pressures and reserve\par accumulation.\par Malaysia\par After a strong rebound in 2002, Malaysia's economy slowed in\par the first half of 2003.  Real GDP grew 3.0 percent in the first\par quarter and 6.1 percent in the second quarter.  The current\par account surplus increased to 12.3 percent of GDP in the first\par half of 2002 from 7.2 percent a year earlier, reflecting\par slowing domestic demand growth.  Malaysia's bilateral trade\par surplus with the United States increased to $6.6 billion in\par 2003H1 from $6.0 billion in 2002H1.\par Malaysia has maintained a fixed peg to the dollar since\par September 1998, when it also imposed capital controls.\par Controls on capital flows have since been relaxed, but offshore\par trading of the ringgit remains prohibited and foreign portfolio\par investment by residents continues to be restricted. The ringgit\par depreciated 2.7 percent over the period on a real\par trade-weighted basis, according to the JP Morgan index.  At the\par end of June, reserves stood at $36.8 billion, up from $34.2\par billion at end-2002."\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031030dzau003pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
