\par\par \b \uc2 MOF's Tanigaki says up to China to decide on yuan.  \b0\par\par\uc2 363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2003\par 09:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday that China itself will have to decide on policy relating to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose peg to the dollar has been criticised for giving Chinese exporters an unfair advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki told China's ambassador to Japan, Wu Dawei, that he wanted the two countries to exchange opinions on the subject regularly, according to a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official who briefed reporters after a 20-minute meeting between the two.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a matter that China must wisely judge for itself," Tanigaki was quoted as telling Wu.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. manufacturers in particular say China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which pegs the yuan at about 8.28 to the dollar, gives an unfair trade advantage to China and kills U.S. jobs.  \par \par \uc2 Some officials in Japan have also expressed concern, saying the peg makes the price of Chinese imported goods low and contributes to Japan's deflation problem.  \par \par \uc2 The issue has come to the fore with the visit to Asia of U.S. President George W. Bush, who faces an election in a year's time and is looking to woo votes among manufacturing workers.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese, concerned about economic stability, have rebuffed Bush, but have agreed to a face-saving plan to set up a joint study group to look into a transition to a market system.  \par \par \uc2 Wu agreed with Tanigaki that the two countries should exchange exchange opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters at every opportunity, the ministry official said. The two did not talk about the U.S.-China study group.  \par \par \uc2 Wu and Tanigaki spent about two minutes on the yuan during their discussion, which also touched on China's economic growth, issues of the country's gap between rich and poor, and development of China's vast and poorer western regions, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's criticism of the yuan peg has been muted because of its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen, from rising and damaging a budding export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been criticised by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031021dzal0013d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
