\par\par \b \uc2 Deflation, markets remain risks for Japan - Takenaka.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Mair  \par \uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2003\par 12:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 12 (Reuters) - The Japanese economy is recovering but it remains fragile with deflation and financial market volatility still posing risks, Japanese Economics and Finance Minister Heizo Takenaka said on Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 Takenaka, attending the World Economic Forum East Asia Summit in Singapore, said the purpose of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mostly to smooth out yen volatility.  \par \par \uc2 "The speed of the change (in the yen) is important," Takenaka told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "If the speed of change is too rapid, this will distort corporate planning regarding investment, marketing, etc. This is the reason why the Minister of Finance is a little bit nervous about the speed of the change."  \par \par \uc2 On Friday the yen surged to a three-year high of 108.28 per dollar after the Bank of Japan eased policy, a move it said was aimed at making an economic recovery more solid.  \par \par \uc2 Takenaka did not comment on the value of the yen, but said a rising yen would take six months to one year to feed through to the real economy, such as by lowering the trade balance.  \par \par \uc2 While the yen has rallied over 8.5 percent since mid-September, other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have lagged, increasing their export competitiveness against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japan and other Asian economies have been strongly criticised by Europe and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  for distorting exchange rates through their heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 GROWTH STRONG, DEFLATION REMAINS  \par \par \uc2 Takenaka also noted that a rise in long term interest rates in the Japanese economy was a potential risk to recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "I welcome of course the rising stock (market) price, but this to some extent is inevitably increasing the long-term (bond) interest rate," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ said on Friday it had no plans to end its ultra-loose monetary policy. Speculation the policy would end has fuelled extreme volatility in Japanese government bonds this year.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, Takenaka told a WEF forum that while strong second quarter economic growth had been reported, deflation remained a serious problem for Japan. He said the government needed to take advantage of the strong growth to accelerate the pace of reform.  \par \par \uc2 "Real GDP is increasing more than we expected. However, on the other hand, deflation is more serious than we had expected," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "So, additional effort is needed between the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This will be another challenge for us," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He said there had been improvements in the level of banks' bad loans and a dramatic improvement in corporate profits.  \par \par \uc2 But the next step of the reform process, which would last two years or so, would be one of intensive adjustment that would keep growth around 0.5 to 1.0 percent per annum.  \par \par \uc2 "But beyond that...we will able to restore potential growth at 2.0 to 2.5 percent," he said, noting there were still many structural problems in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Second quarter GDP figures showed the economy grew at an annualised 3.9 percent - the fastest pace in two and a half years and exceeding the 3.1 percent growth in the United States.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031012dzac005el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
